# HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 over `IyiSocket`, written in iyi: a client, a server,
# and the bodies between them as streams.
#
# **The client.** `HTTP.get`, `head`, `post`, `put`, `delete` and the
# `request` under them speak `http://` and `https://`, to a host name
# (resolved as `IyiSocket.connect` resolves one, through iyi's own
# resolver), an IPv4 address, an IPv6 one in brackets
# (`http://[::1]:8080/`) or `localhost`. `https://` is std/tls's TLS 1.3,
# the server's certificate checked for the host against the system's roots
# or `HTTP.tls_config`'s. Each answers the whole `Response`; `HTTP.open(...) {
# |response| ... }` answers the block's value instead, the body still on
# the wire: `response.body_io.read` takes it a piece at a time, so a
# download of any size costs what one piece costs. A request's body is a
# `String`, sent with its length, or an `IyiIO`, sent chunked as it is
# read. Redirects (301, 302, 303, 307, 308) are followed, ten at most by
# default, the method becoming GET where a browser's does; credentials and
# cookies a caller wrote are not carried to another host. A body coded
# `gzip`, `deflate` or `zstd` (RFC 8878, its window held to the 8 MB RFC
# 9659 sets) is decoded as it arrives, unless the caller asked for a
# coding itself; a `jar:` (std/cookie's `CookieJar`) keeps what
# `Set-Cookie` sets and sends it back. Each `HTTP` request is a
# connection of its own; an `HTTPClient` keeps the connections its
# answers leave open and sends the next request to that server over one.
# Response headers are read by name without regard to case, a field on
# several lines is one field with its values joined by ", "
# (`header_values` keeps the lines apart, as a `Set-Cookie` must be
# read), and interim 1xx answers are read past.
#
# **The server.** `Server.serve(listener) { |request| Response }`: a
# fiber per connection under a group, each connection kept alive across
# requests until the client or the block's answer says `Connection:
# close`, the client closes, or sends something that is not HTTP
# (answered 400 and closed; a body past `Server::MAX_BODY` 413, a head
# past `Server::MAX_HEAD` 431). The block gets a `Request` - method, path
# (an absolute-form target's own), query, headers, body - and answers a
# `Response`. By default the body is read whole before the block runs;
# with `stream_bodies: true` the block reads `request.body_io` itself, and
# what it leaves unread is read past, up to a megabyte, or the connection
# closed. An answer is a `String`, written with its length; an `IyiIO`,
# copied out and closed (with its length when the block gave a
# `Content-Length`, chunked otherwise); a `Response.stream { |w| ... }`
# whose block writes as it goes; or a `Response.upgrade`, after whose 101
# the connection belongs to the block (std/websocket's). With `compress:
# true` an answer whose type is worth compressing goes `zstd` or `gzip`
# to a client that accepts one: zstd for a whole body when the client's
# `q` for it is at least its `q` for gzip, gzip for a stream
# (`Server.coding_for`). Beside it: std/router routes requests by
# pattern as Go's ServeMux does, std/fileserver answers them from a
# directory, std/sse streams events, and std/websocket takes a
# connection over.
# The socket parks under the runtime, so a slow client costs one fiber;
# the listener's `close` ends `serve`. With `tls:` a `TlsServerConfig`
# (std/tls's server: TLS 1.3, and 1.2 when its `min_version` asks; an
# Ed25519, P-256, P-384 or RSA certificate) every connection is TLS, its
# handshake first and close_notify last; inside it, as in the clear, a
# connection that opens with HTTP/2's preface - as one whose ALPN chose
# `h2` does - is served HTTP/2, any other HTTP/1.1.
# A config with `client_roots` asks each client for its certificate, and
# a request carries the chain it presented in `peer_certificates`; an
# `https://` request presents `HTTP.tls_config`'s `identity` when asked.
# No early data (0-RTT), either way: a server here takes none whatever its
# config allows - its clients send the request again after the handshake -
# and the client keeps no session, so it sends none. A request that runs
# twice when replayed (a POST) must never go early, and HTTP cannot tell
# a GET with side effects from one without.
#
# **Errors are values.** What the other end does wrong - a malformed head,
# a body cut short, a broken chunk, a corrupt gzip - is an `HTTPError`
# beside `Cancelled` and `SocketError`; `!` passes it up and `.or_panic`
# refuses it with its sentence. What a program does wrong - a header with
# a line break in it, a status of 2000 - is a panic, before anything is
# written. `format_request`, `parse_request`, `format_response` and
# `parse_response` are the text of whole messages, pure, for a program
# and for the gate; both halves are this one module, so what one writes
# the other reads, and `bench/std_http_exercise.sh` runs the client
# against the server and both against Python's.
#
# **HTTP/2** (RFC 9113, the section near the end of this file): the same
# requests and answers as frames over one connection. `serve` takes a
# connection that opens with HTTP/2's preface as HTTP/2 (h2c with prior
# knowledge) beside HTTP/1.1 on the same listener; the client speaks it
# where TLS's ALPN chose `h2` - offered once a program lists it in
# `HTTP.tls_config.alpn` - and over `http://` from an
# `HTTPClient.new(prior_knowledge: true)`. A server's block, a request's
# body and an answer's are what they are over HTTP/1.1; `version` says
# `HTTP/2`. bench/http2_exercise.iyi and the gate hold it against curl,
# Node's own HTTP/2 both ways (TLS with ALPN included, the server's own
# TLS too) and RFC 9113's error rules frame by frame.
#
# **Through a proxy.** `proxy:` on `HTTP.request`, `HTTP.open` and
# `HTTPClient.new` is an `http://` proxy's URL (`host:port` alone will
# do), credentials and all: `http://` goes to it as an absolute-form
# request (RFC 9112 §3.2.2), `https://` through a `CONNECT` tunnel (RFC
# 9110 §9.3.6) with TLS to the server inside, and the URL's user and
# password go as `Proxy-Authorization: Basic` to the proxy alone, never
# inside a tunnel. `HTTP.upgrade(proxy:)` - std/websocket's
# `WebSocket.connect` - goes through a tunnel for `http://` too, its
# handshake inside (`upgrade` says why). A CONNECT the proxy refuses
# (407, 403, 502) is an `HTTPError` naming its status; kept connections
# are kept by proxy, and a tunnel by proxy and server.
# `HTTP.proxy_for(url, http_proxy, https_proxy, no_proxy)` says which
# proxy a URL takes under those variables' values, `NO_PROXY` read as
# Go's `httpproxy` reads it, and std/http_proxy's `HTTPProxy.from_env`
# reads them from the environment. bench/std_http_exercise.sh holds it
# against a proxy written in Python - a WebSocket through it, `ws://`
# and `wss://`, among the rest - curl through the same proxy, and Go's
# net/http for `NO_PROXY`.
#
# **What it reaches.** What std/socket reaches, and for `https://` what
# std/tls does - the operating system's trusted roots and its entropy -
# and nothing of its own: no file, and no clock or environment beyond
# those. Cookies with their expiry are std/cookie's, a directory served is
# std/fileserver's, a multipart boundary is made from the parts' own
# hash, and a proxy named by the environment is std/http_proxy's to read.
#
# Not here: HTTP/2's server push, its upgrade from HTTP/1.1 (which RFC
# 9113 retired) and RFC 8441's WebSocket over HTTP/2 - an upgrade offers
# ALPN `http/1.1` alone - pipelining requests on a kept HTTP/1.1
# connection; a proxy reached over TLS (`https://`) or SOCKS, which are
# refused by name; and a streamed answer in zstd, std/zstd's compressor
# taking its input whole.

module std/http

import std/socket::{IyiSocket, SocketError}
import std/tls::{TlsClient, TlsConfig, TlsConnection, TlsError, TlsIdentity, TlsServer, TlsServerConfig, TlsVersion}
import std/x509::{Certificate}
import std/uri::{URI, Params}
import std/mime::{MIME}
import std/compress::{Inflater, Deflater, Envelope, Gzip, CompressError}
# HTTP's third content coding (RFC 9659), read as it arrives and written
# for a whole answer.
import std/zstd::{ZstdReader, ZstdError, Zstd}
import std/digest::{SHA1}
# A proxy's `Proxy-Authorization: Basic`.
import std/base64::{Base64}
# A host beyond ASCII lowered for NO_PROXY's comparison; std/uri's IDNA
# brings it already.
import std/unicode::{Unicode}
# HTTP/2's header compression (RFC 7541), named in full where it is used,
# and the byte slices it reads and writes.
import std/hpack
import std/slice
# The clock a deadline is read on. std/time's, not the runtime's hook: the
# clock is std/time's to reach, and a module that reads it through its own
# hook call is counted by `iyi mod reach` as reaching past the language.
import std/time::{Time}

# What the other end did that HTTP does not allow, as a value: `reason` is
# the sentence, `status` the answer a server gives a request that did it
# (400, or 413 and 431 for the limits). `message` says it is HTTP's.
pub struct HTTPError
  getter reason : String
  getter status : Int32

  def initialize(@reason : String, @status : Int32 = 400)
  end

  def to_s : String
    "HTTP: " + @reason
  end
end

impl Error for HTTPError
  def message : String
    "HTTP: " + @reason
  end
end

# The bytes a connection has delivered that nothing has taken yet, and the
# socket the next come from. Heads, chunk sizes and bodies are all read
# out of this one buffer, where they lie: requests that came in one read
# are parsed in place, and a head that comes a piece at a time is searched
# only where the new piece could end it. It grows for a head as large as
# the limit allows, and otherwise stays the size of a read.
#
# Under `serve`, *listener* and *idle*: no read starts once the listener
# is closed, and every read waits with the connection in *idle*, for
# `serve` to end (`close_for_stop`) when the listener closes - one partway
# through a request as much as one between requests.
class Conn
  @socket : IyiSocket
  @data : Pointer(UInt8)
  @capacity : Int32
  @start : Int32
  @stop : Int32
  # Where a head's blank line may still begin: the bytes before it have
  # been searched.
  @searched : Int32
  @ended : Bool
  @failure : Cancelled | SocketError | Nil
  @listener : IyiSocket?
  @idle : Hash(UInt64, Conn)?
  # Handed to an upgrade's protocol (`held`), which writes when it likes.
  @handed_over : Bool = false
  # When what is being read must have arrived by (monotonic ns, 0 for no
  # such time) and what it is, and how long one read may wait otherwise.
  @deadline : Int64 = 0_i64
  @deadline_what : String = "read a request's head"
  @gap : Int32? = nil
  # A client's `https://` connection: TLS over the socket, which every
  # read and write then goes through. The socket's timeouts are TLS's too.
  @stream : TlsStream? = nil
  # The HTTP/2 connection over it, when the two ends speak that.
  property h2 : H2Connection? = nil
  # What an `https://` connection's handshake was made under, for a
  # client to tell whether it may carry a request made under another.
  property made_under : TlsMadeUnder? = nil

  ROOM = 16384

  def initialize(@socket : IyiSocket, @listener : IyiSocket? = nil, @idle : Hash(UInt64, Conn)? = nil, @stream : TlsStream? = nil)
    @capacity = ROOM
    @data = Pointer(UInt8).malloc(@capacity.to_u64)
    @start = 0
    @stop = 0
    @searched = 0
    @ended = false
    @failure = nil
  end

  def socket : IyiSocket
    @socket
  end

  # How long a stopping server's close_notify may wait to be written: a
  # client that reads nothing, its socket's buffer full, holds the stop
  # this long and no longer.
  STOP_WRITE_MS = 100

  # What `serve` does to a connection waiting in a read when its listener
  # closes: over TLS, close_notify, then the socket closed (`TlsConnection
  # #close`), so a client reads the end of the connection rather than a
  # truncation; in the clear, the socket closed. Either ends the read. A
  # connection an upgrade handed over is the protocol's to write on, from
  # its own task, and has its socket closed alone. The socket closed under
  # it, the connection's own task finds it so: TLS answers that it is
  # closed, and HTTP/2's GOAWAY, after a read that failed, is not written.
  def close_for_stop : Nil
    if (stream = @stream) && !@handed_over && !@socket.closed?
      @socket.write_timeout_ms = STOP_WRITE_MS
      stream.close
    end
    @socket.close
  end

  # What a request is written to: TLS over the socket, or the socket.
  def transport : IyiSocket | TlsStream
    @stream || @socket
  end

  def failure : Cancelled | SocketError | Nil
    @failure
  end

  # Whether the other end closed its side.
  def ended? : Bool
    @ended
  end

  def available : Int32
    @stop - @start
  end

  # The byte *at* bytes past what has been taken, which must have arrived.
  def peek(at : Int32) : UInt8
    @data[@start + at]
  end

  def consume(count : Int32) : Nil
    @start = @start + count
    @searched = @start if @searched < @start
  end

  # Up to *max* of the bytes that have arrived, taken; none are read.
  def take(max : Int32) : String
    count = available < max ? available : max
    piece = String.new(count) do |buf|
      buf.copy_from(@data + @start.to_i64, count)
    end
    consume(count)
    piece
  end

  # Everything that has arrived and nothing has taken: what a connection
  # handed to another protocol starts with.
  def leftover : String
    take(available)
  end

  # One read appended to what has arrived. False at the other end's close,
  # on a failure (kept in `failure`), and under `serve` once the listener
  # is closed.
  def fill : Bool
    return false if @ended || !@failure.nil?
    if listener = @listener
      return false if listener.closed?
    end
    make_room
    if @deadline > 0_i64
      left = (@deadline - Time.monotonic_nanoseconds.to_i64) // 1000000_i64
      if left <= 0_i64
        @failure = SocketError.timed_out(@deadline_what)
        return false
      end
      # The pause a read may take still holds inside the deadline.
      if gap = @gap
        left = gap.to_i64 if gap > 0 && gap.to_i64 < left
      end
      @socket.read_timeout_ms = left.to_i32
    end
    if idle = @idle
      idle[@socket.object_id] = self
    end
    stream = @stream
    got = stream ? stream.read(@data + @stop.to_i64, @capacity - @stop) : @socket.read(@data + @stop.to_i64, @capacity - @stop)
    if idle = @idle
      idle.delete(@socket.object_id)
    end
    case got
    in Int32
      if got == 0
        @ended = true
        return false
      end
      @stop = @stop + got
      true
    in Cancelled
      @failure = got
      false
    in SocketError
      @failure = got
      false
    end
  end

  # Each read may wait *gap* ms, and the socket's writes as long (nil: as
  # long as it takes).
  def gap=(gap : Int32?) : Int32?
    @socket.read_timeout_ms = gap
    @socket.write_timeout_ms = gap
    @gap = gap
  end

  # What arrives from here must have arrived within *ms* (0: no limit),
  # however it is spread over reads: a read's own limit is a pause, and a
  # client that sends a byte a little inside it holds a connection for as
  # long as it likes.
  def deadline_in(ms : Int32) : Nil
    deadline_at(ms > 0 ? Time.monotonic_nanoseconds.to_i64 + ms.to_i64 * 1000000_i64 : 0_i64)
  end

  # The same, by the monotonic clock's nanoseconds (0: no limit); *what*
  # is what the timed-out read says it was doing.
  def deadline_at(ns : Int64, what : String = "read a request's head") : Nil
    @deadline = ns
    @deadline_what = what
  end

  def no_deadline : Nil
    @deadline = 0_i64
    @socket.read_timeout_ms = @gap
  end

  # The connection is someone else's protocol's now, which waits as it
  # likes: no deadline and no limit on a read or a write.
  def unlimited : Nil
    @deadline = 0_i64
    self.gap = nil
  end

  # Reads until *count* bytes have arrived; false when they cannot.
  def need(count : Int32) : Bool
    while available < count
      return false unless fill
    end
    true
  end

  # Room for a read: what has been taken moves out of the way, and a
  # buffer full of what has not doubles.
  private def make_room : Nil
    if @start == @stop
      @start = 0
      @stop = 0
      @searched = 0
    elsif @capacity - @stop < ROOM // 4 && @start > 0
      held = @stop - @start
      # Down, front to back: no byte is written before it is read.
      @data.copy_from(@data + @start.to_i64, held)
      @searched = @searched - @start
      @searched = 0 if @searched < 0
      @stop = held
      @start = 0
    end
    if @capacity - @stop < ROOM // 4
      @capacity = @capacity * 2
      @data = @data.realloc(@capacity.to_u64)
    end
  end

  # A head: what comes before the next blank line, taken with it. Empty
  # lines before it are skipped when *blank_first* (RFC 9112 §2.2 has a
  # server ignore them). Nil when the connection ends first - `failure`
  # says whether it failed, and `available` whether part of a head had
  # come - and an error past *limit* bytes.
  def read_head(limit : Int32, what : String, blank_first : Bool = false) : String | HTTPError | Nil
    while true
      if blank_first
        while available >= 2 && peek(0) == 13_u8 && peek(1) == 10_u8
          consume(2)
        end
        if available == 1 && peek(0) == 13_u8
          return nil unless fill
          next
        end
      end
      from = @searched - 3
      from = @start if from < @start
      at = from
      while at + 3 < @stop
        if @data[at] == 13_u8 && @data[at + 1] == 10_u8 && @data[at + 2] == 13_u8 && @data[at + 3] == 10_u8
          head = String.new(at - @start) do |buf|
            buf.copy_from(@data + @start.to_i64, at - @start)
          end
          consume(at + 4 - @start)
          return head
        end
        at = at + 1
      end
      @searched = @stop
      return HTTPError.new("#{what} headers past #{limit} bytes", 431) if available > limit
      return nil unless fill
    end
  end

  # A line ended by CRLF, taken with it: a chunk's size, a trailer. An
  # error past *limit* bytes; nil when the connection ends first. Where
  # the search has reached is kept from the start of what is held, since
  # a read moves what is held to the front of the buffer: kept as a
  # place in the buffer, it pointed past the line once a chunk's size
  # arrived apart from its CRLF, and the body was read as cut short.
  def read_line(limit : Int32, what : String) : String | HTTPError | Nil
    searched = 0
    while true
      at = @start + searched
      while at + 1 < @stop
        if @data[at] == 13_u8 && @data[at + 1] == 10_u8
          line = String.new(at - @start) do |buf|
            buf.copy_from(@data + @start.to_i64, at - @start)
          end
          consume(at + 2 - @start)
          return line
        end
        at = at + 1
      end
      searched = at - @start
      return HTTPError.new("#{what} past #{limit} bytes") if available > limit
      return nil unless fill
    end
  end

  # *block* run with the connection counted among the idle ones, which a
  # close of the listener closes: an upgraded connection's protocol reads
  # it for as long as it lasts, and `serve` waited for that - a WebSocket
  # client that stayed kept `serve` from returning after its listener was
  # closed, for as long as it stayed. Go's server does not wait for a
  # connection it handed over; this one cannot leave a task behind, so it
  # closes the connection under the protocol, whose next read answers that
  # it closed, and whose `closed?` says so: `IyiSocket#write` refuses a
  # closed socket by panicking, so a protocol asks first, as std/websocket
  # does, while over TLS a write answers it as an error too. A listener
  # closed before the block starts closes the connection at once.
  def held(& : -> Nil) : Nil
    idle = @idle
    listener = @listener
    if idle && listener
      @handed_over = true
      if listener.closed?
        @socket.close
      else
        idle[@socket.object_id] = self
      end
    end
    yield
    idle.delete(@socket.object_id) if idle
  end

  # A connection speaking HTTP/2 says GOAWAY first, so the other end knows
  # no request was lost in the close.
  def close : Nil
    if h2 = @h2
      h2.goaway
    end
    if stream = @stream
      stream.close
    else
      @socket.close
    end
  end
end

# The `TlsConfig` an `https://` connection was made under, and the parts of
# it that decide whom the connection trusts and what it presented: the
# roots, the identity, the time certificates were checked at and the
# oldest version spoken. A kept connection is handed out only for a
# request whose `HTTP.tls_config` is the same object holding the same
# ones, so a connection checked against one trust store never carries a
# request made under another - set by `HTTP.tls_config=`, or by assigning
# `roots` or `identity` on the configuration in place. Objects are told
# apart by `object_id`, and the references are held, so an address freed
# and handed to a new configuration is never taken for the old one.
# Certificates added to the same roots array in place are not seen: such
# an array is still the same object.
class TlsMadeUnder
  @config : TlsConfig
  @roots : Array(Certificate)?
  @identity : TlsIdentity?
  @now : Time?
  @min_version : TlsVersion

  def initialize(@config : TlsConfig)
    @roots = @config.roots
    @identity = @config.identity
    @now = @config.now
    @min_version = @config.min_version
  end

  def same?(config : TlsConfig) : Bool
    return false unless @config.object_id == config.object_id
    roots = config.roots
    held = @roots
    return false unless held.nil? ? roots.nil? : (!roots.nil? && held.object_id == roots.object_id)
    identity = config.identity
    mine = @identity
    return false unless mine.nil? ? identity.nil? : (!identity.nil? && mine.object_id == identity.object_id)
    @now == config.now && @min_version == config.min_version
  end
end

# A TLS connection used as a socket is: read into a buffer, written to,
# closed, its timeouts set - by a `Conn`, a `BodyWriter`, and a connection
# handed over by an upgrade (`wss://` at either end). What TLS refuses is a
# `SocketError` carrying TLS's own sentence (`layer_reason`), so answers
# keep the types they have over a plain socket.
pub class TlsStream
  @tls : TlsConnection
  @host : String
  # What TLS handed over and the buffer has not taken yet.
  @pending : String = ""
  @at : Int32 = 0

  def initialize(@tls : TlsConnection, @host : String)
  end

  # The peer's certificate chain, leaf first, as the handshake checked it:
  # the server's, at a client; the client's, at a server that asked.
  def peer_certificates : Array(Certificate)
    @tls.peer_certificates
  end

  # Up to *count* bytes into *buffer*: 0 once the peer closed with
  # close_notify.
  def read(buffer : Pointer(UInt8), count : Int32) : Int32 | Cancelled | SocketError
    if @at >= @pending.bytesize
      got = @tls.read(16384)
      return failed_read(got) if got.is_a?(TlsError)
      return 0 if got.empty?
      @pending = got
      @at = 0
    end
    n = @pending.bytesize - @at
    n = count if n > count
    buffer.copy_from(@pending.to_unsafe + @at.to_i64, n)
    @at = @at + n
    n
  end

  # Up to *max_bytes* as a string, what is held first: "" once the peer
  # closed with close_notify. `IyiSocket#read`'s form, for a block an
  # upgrade hands either.
  def read(max_bytes : Int32 = 4096) : String | Cancelled | SocketError
    if @at < @pending.bytesize
      n = @pending.bytesize - @at
      n = max_bytes if n > max_bytes
      from = @at
      piece = String.new(n) { |buf| buf.copy_from(@pending.to_unsafe + from.to_i64, n) }
      @at = @at + n
      return piece
    end
    got = @tls.read(max_bytes)
    return failed_read(got) if got.is_a?(TlsError)
    got
  end

  # A read TLS refused, as a socket's would be: a wait past its timeout is
  # `timed_out?` and a cancelled one `Cancelled` - the connection is open
  # after either - and anything else carries TLS's sentence.
  private def failed_read(error : TlsError) : Cancelled | SocketError
    return Cancelled.new if error.cancelled?
    return SocketError.timed_out("read from #{@host}") if error.timed_out?
    SocketError.layered("read from #{@host}", error.message)
  end

  def write(text : String) : Int32 | Cancelled | SocketError
    sent = @tls.write(text)
    return SocketError.layered("write to #{@host}", sent.message) if sent.is_a?(TlsError)
    sent
  end

  def close : Nil
    @tls.close
  end

  def closed? : Bool
    @tls.closed?
  end

  # How long a read or a write of the connection under it may wait.
  def read_timeout_ms=(ms : Int32?) : Int32?
    @tls.read_timeout_ms = ms
  end

  def write_timeout_ms=(ms : Int32?) : Int32?
    @tls.write_timeout_ms = ms
  end
end

# A body as it arrives: from a connection, framed by its length, by chunks
# or by the connection's close, or from a string already in hand. `read`
# answers the next piece, "" once the body is done; what the other end
# does wrong is an `HTTPError`, and a connection that fails or is
# cancelled answers that. The first failure is kept: every read after it
# answers the same. A body coded `gzip`, `deflate` or `zstd` is decoded
# as it is read when the client asked for that.
#
# A body has a limit: past it a read answers an `HTTPError` with status
# 413. The server's is `Server::MAX_BODY`; a client's is none.
pub class BodyReader
  EMPTY   = 0
  LENGTH  = 1
  CHUNKED = 2
  CLOSE   = 3
  TEXT    = 4
  # An HTTP/2 stream's DATA frames.
  FRAMES = 5

  @conn : Conn?
  @frames : H2Connection? = nil
  @frames_id : Int32 = 0
  @kind : Int32
  # LENGTH: bytes still to come. CHUNKED: bytes left of this chunk, -1
  # when a size line comes next.
  @left : Int64
  @declared : Int64
  @limit : Int64
  @total : Int64
  @finished : Bool
  @fault : HTTPError | Cancelled | SocketError | Nil
  @text : String
  @at : Int32
  # Called once, before the first read that has to wait on the socket: a
  # server answers `Expect: 100-continue` there, and not before the body
  # is wanted.
  @hello : Proc(Bool)?
  # The coding undone as the body is read (`decode`, `decode_zstd`): the
  # stream, and until it starts, the envelope it was asked for and the
  # bytes read to tell deflate's two forms apart.
  @inflater : Inflater?
  @coding : Envelope?
  @first : String?
  @zstd : ZstdReader? = nil

  def initialize(@conn : Conn?, @kind : Int32, length : Int64, @limit : Int64, @text : String = "", hello : Proc(Bool)? = nil)
    @left = @kind == CHUNKED ? -1_i64 : length
    @declared = length
    @total = 0_i64
    @finished = @kind == EMPTY || (@kind == LENGTH && length == 0_i64) || (@kind == TEXT && @text.bytesize == 0)
    @fault = nil
    @at = 0
    @hello = hello
    @inflater = nil
    @coding = nil
    @first = nil
  end

  # A body that is a string already in hand.
  def self.text(text : String) : BodyReader
    BodyReader.new(nil, TEXT, text.bytesize.to_i64, 9223372036854775807_i64, text)
  end

  # A body that arrives as an HTTP/2 stream's DATA: refused past *limit*.
  protected def self.frames(h2 : H2Connection, id : Int32, limit : Int64) : BodyReader
    reader = BodyReader.new(nil, FRAMES, 0_i64, limit)
    reader.on_frames(h2, id)
    reader
  end

  protected def on_frames(h2 : H2Connection, id : Int32) : Nil
    @frames = h2
    @frames_id = id
  end

  # Whether the body ends where the connection does: nothing can follow
  # it on the connection.
  def to_close? : Bool
    @kind == CLOSE
  end

  # Whether every byte of the body has been read, the framing's end too.
  def finished? : Bool
    return false unless @finished && @coding.nil?
    if inflater = @inflater
      return inflater.finished?
    end
    if zstd = @zstd
      return zstd.finished?
    end
    true
  end

  # The body's coding undone from here on: `Envelope::Gzip` for gzip,
  # `Envelope::Zlib` for HTTP's deflate - which some servers send as the
  # bare stream instead, without zlib's two bytes of header, so the first
  # two bytes are looked at first, and a pair that is not a zlib header
  # (RFC 1950 §2.2: method 8, a multiple of 31) is read as the bare
  # stream. A body that does not decode is an `HTTPError` naming the
  # coding and why.
  def decode(envelope : Envelope) : Nil
    @coding = envelope
  end

  # The body's `zstd` coding (RFC 8878) undone from here on, by std/zstd's
  # `ZstdReader` as the pieces arrive, frames one after another. A frame
  # that asks for a window past *max_window* bytes is refused rather than
  # given the memory (a window outside 1 KiB to 512 MiB is a panic), and a
  # body that does not decode is an `HTTPError` naming the coding and why.
  def decode_zstd(max_window : Int32 = Zstd::WINDOW_LIMIT) : Nil
    @zstd = ZstdReader.new(max_window) { feed }
  end

  # The next piece of the body, at most *max* bytes; "" when it is done.
  def read(max : Int32 = 65536) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    if fault = @fault
      return fault
    end
    raise "BodyReader: a read of #{max} bytes" if max < 1
    if coding = @coding
      @coding = nil
      started = start_inflater(coding)
      return started unless started.nil?
    end
    if inflater = @inflater
      return decoded(inflater.read(max))
    end
    if zstd = @zstd
      return decoded(zstd.read(max))
    end
    raw(max)
  end

  # A decoder's answer: its piece, or once its stream has ended the
  # framing's end read for, or its refusal as the body's.
  private def decoded(got : String | CompressError | ZstdError) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    if got.is_a?(String)
      return got if got.bytesize > 0
      # The coded stream has ended; the framing has not until its last
      # chunk or byte is read, and a kept connection's next answer
      # starts after it. What follows the stream inside the body is
      # passed over, as zlib's own reader does with what follows a
      # gzip member it cannot read.
      until @finished
        rest = raw(65536)
        return rest unless rest.is_a?(String)
      end
      return ""
    end
    if fault = @fault
      return fault
    end
    remember(HTTPError.new("the body's coding: " + got.message))
  end

  private def start_inflater(coding : Envelope) : Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError | Nil
    envelope = coding
    if coding == Envelope::Zlib
      first = ""
      while first.bytesize < 2
        got = raw(65536)
        return got unless got.is_a?(String)
        break if got.bytesize == 0
        first = first + got
      end
      p = first.to_unsafe
      zlib = first.bytesize >= 2 && (p[0] & 15_u8) == 8_u8 && ((p[0].to_i32 << 8) | p[1].to_i32) % 31 == 0
      envelope = Envelope::Raw unless zlib
      @first = first
    end
    @inflater = Inflater.new(envelope) { feed }
    nil
  end

  # The inflater's source: the bytes read to sniff the envelope, then the
  # framing's, nil at its end or when it fails - the failure is kept, and
  # answered in place of the inflater's "ends early".
  private def feed : String?
    if first = @first
      @first = nil
      return first if first.bytesize > 0
    end
    got = raw(65536)
    unless got.is_a?(String)
      @fault = got
      return nil
    end
    got.bytesize == 0 ? nil : got
  end

  # The rest of the body as one string, refused past *limit* bytes (413).
  # The pieces are kept as they arrive and joined once: a builder of the
  # length a head declared was made before any of it had come, and a head
  # that declared 64 MB and sent two bytes cost the server 64 MB.
  def read_all(limit : Int64 = 2147483647_i64) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    pieces = [] of String
    total = 0_i64
    while true
      got = read(65536)
      return got unless got.is_a?(String)
      break if got.bytesize == 0
      total = total + got.bytesize.to_i64
      if total > limit
        return remember(HTTPError.new("body past #{limit} bytes", 413))
      end
      pieces << got
    end
    return "" if pieces.empty?
    return pieces[0] if pieces.size == 1
    String.new(total.to_i32) do |whole|
      at = 0
      pieces.each do |piece|
        (whole + at.to_i64).copy_from(piece.to_unsafe, piece.bytesize)
        at = at + piece.bytesize
      end
      nil
    end
  end

  # Reads past the rest of the body, *limit* bytes at most: whether it
  # ended within them. A server reads past what a block left unread, so
  # the next request on the connection starts where it should.
  def drain(limit : Int64) : Bool
    seen = 0_i64
    while true
      got = read(65536)
      return false unless got.is_a?(String)
      return true if got.bytesize == 0
      seen = seen + got.bytesize.to_i64
      return false if seen > limit
    end
  end

  protected def remember(fault : HTTPError | Cancelled | SocketError) : HTTPError | Cancelled | SocketError
    @fault = fault
    fault
  end

  # The body's bytes as the framing delivers them, before any decoding.
  protected def raw(max : Int32) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    return "" if @finished
    case @kind
    when TEXT
      take = @text.bytesize - @at
      take = max if take > max
      piece = @text[@at, take]
      @at = @at + take
      @finished = true if @at == @text.bytesize
      piece
    when LENGTH
      conn = connection
      return short(conn) unless arrived(conn)
      want = conn.available.to_i64
      want = @left if want > @left
      want = max.to_i64 if want > max.to_i64
      piece = conn.take(want.to_i32)
      @left = @left - piece.bytesize.to_i64
      @total = @total + piece.bytesize.to_i64
      @finished = true if @left == 0_i64
      piece
    when CHUNKED
      conn = connection
      if @left == -1_i64
        size = chunk_size(conn)
        return size unless size.is_a?(Int64)
        if size == 0_i64
          return trailers(conn)
        end
        if size > @limit - @total
          return remember(HTTPError.new("chunked body past #{@limit} bytes", 413))
        end
        @left = size
      end
      unless arrived(conn)
        return failed(conn) || remember(HTTPError.new("chunked body ends inside a chunk"))
      end
      want = conn.available.to_i64
      want = @left if want > @left
      want = max.to_i64 if want > max.to_i64
      piece = conn.take(want.to_i32)
      @left = @left - piece.bytesize.to_i64
      @total = @total + piece.bytesize.to_i64
      if @left == 0_i64
        unless conn.need(2)
          return failed(conn) || remember(HTTPError.new("chunked body ends inside a chunk"))
        end
        unless conn.peek(0) == 13_u8 && conn.peek(1) == 10_u8
          return remember(HTTPError.new("chunked body: a chunk is not followed by CRLF"))
        end
        conn.consume(2)
        @left = -1_i64
      end
      piece
    when CLOSE
      conn = connection
      unless arrived(conn)
        if fault = conn.failure
          return remember(fault)
        end
        @finished = true
        return ""
      end
      want = conn.available
      want = max if want > max
      piece = conn.take(want)
      @total = @total + piece.bytesize.to_i64
      if @total > @limit
        return remember(HTTPError.new("body past #{@limit} bytes", 413))
      end
      piece
    when FRAMES
      h2 = @frames
      raise "BodyReader: an HTTP/2 body on no connection" unless h2
      got = h2.body_piece(@frames_id, max)
      return remember(got) unless got.is_a?(String)
      if got.bytesize == 0
        @finished = true
        return ""
      end
      @total = @total + got.bytesize.to_i64
      return remember(HTTPError.new("body past #{@limit} bytes", 413)) if @total > @limit
      got
    else
      @finished = true
      ""
    end
  end

  private def connection : Conn
    conn = @conn
    raise "BodyReader: a body on no connection" unless conn
    conn
  end

  # Whether some of the body has arrived to be taken, reading for it.
  private def arrived(conn : Conn) : Bool
    return true if conn.available > 0
    if hello = @hello
      @hello = nil
      unless hello.call
        return false
      end
    end
    conn.fill
  end

  private def short(conn : Conn) : Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    failed(conn) || remember(HTTPError.new("the body ended after #{@declared - @left} of its #{@declared} bytes"))
  end

  private def failed(conn : Conn) : Cancelled | SocketError | Nil
    fault = conn.failure
    return nil if fault.nil?
    @fault = fault
    fault
  end

  # A chunk's size line (RFC 9112 §7.1): hexadecimal, extensions after
  # `;` ignored.
  private def chunk_size(conn : Conn) : Int64 | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    if hello = @hello
      if conn.available == 0
        @hello = nil
        return remember(HTTPError.new("the connection failed before the body")) unless hello.call
      end
    end
    line = conn.read_line(4096, "a chunk size line")
    case line
    in Nil
      failed(conn) || remember(HTTPError.new("chunked body ends before its last chunk"))
    in HTTPError
      remember(line)
    in String
      text = line
      if semi = text.index(';')
        text = text[0, semi]
      end
      size = HTTP.hex(HTTP.trim(text))
      return remember(HTTPError.new("chunked body: not a chunk size: #{line.inspect}")) unless size
      size.to_i64
    end
  end

  # What follows the last chunk: trailers, which nothing here reads, and
  # the blank line that ends them.
  private def trailers(conn : Conn) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    while true
      line = conn.read_line(4096, "trailers")
      case line
      in Nil
        return failed(conn) || remember(HTTPError.new("chunked body ends inside its trailers"))
      in HTTPError
        return remember(line)
      in String
        if line.bytesize == 0
          @finished = true
          return ""
        end
      end
    end
    ""
  end
end

# What a streamed body is written to: an answer's `Response.stream`
# block's, and a client's upload from an `IyiIO`. Each `write` goes out as
# one chunk (RFC 9112 §7.1), or as it is where the connection's close ends
# the body, so a block that writes a line a second is read a line a
# second. Under a server's `compress`, each write is a gzip piece flushed
# whole (std/compress's `Deflater`), which decodes as it arrives too. The
# first failure is kept, and every write after it answers it; `finish`,
# which the server calls when the block returns, writes the end.
pub class BodyWriter
  @socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream | H2Out
  @chunked : Bool
  @deflater : Deflater?
  @fault : Cancelled | SocketError | Nil
  @written : Int64
  @done : Bool

  def initialize(@socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream | H2Out, @chunked : Bool, @deflater : Deflater? = nil)
    @fault = nil
    @written = 0_i64
    @done = false
  end

  # Bytes of the body written so far, before any framing or coding.
  def written : Int64
    @written
  end

  def write(text : String) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError
    if fault = @fault
      return fault
    end
    raise "BodyWriter: a write after the body's end" if @done
    return nil if text.bytesize == 0
    @written = @written + text.bytesize.to_i64
    if deflater = @deflater
      return put(deflater.compress(text))
    end
    put(text)
  end

  # The end of the body: the gzip trailer, and the last chunk with the
  # blank line after it. Once; the server's call after a block that
  # finished its own body writes nothing.
  def finish : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError
    if fault = @fault
      return fault
    end
    return nil if @done
    @done = true
    if deflater = @deflater
      put(deflater.finish)
    end
    send("0\r\n\r\n") if @chunked && @fault.nil?
    @fault
  end

  # One chunk in one write when it is small - a write each for the size,
  # the bytes and the CRLF cost three system calls a chunk - and the bytes
  # apart when it is large, rather than copied to join them.
  private def put(text : String) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError
    return nil if text.bytesize == 0
    if @chunked
      size = HTTP.hex_text(text.bytesize.to_i64)
      if text.bytesize <= 16384
        send(size + "\r\n" + text + "\r\n")
      else
        return @fault unless send(size + "\r\n")
        return @fault unless send(text)
        send("\r\n")
      end
    else
      send(text)
    end
    @fault
  end

  private def send(text : String) : Bool
    got = @socket.write(text)
    case got
    in Int32
      true
    in Cancelled
      @fault = got
      false
    in SocketError
      @fault = got
      false
    end
  end
end

# What a server's block is handed: one request. Its body is `body`, read
# whole before the block ran, or under `stream_bodies` read by the block
# from `body_io` as it arrives; `body` then reads the rest itself, and a
# body that cannot be read - the client went, or sent one that is not
# HTTP - ends the connection with that sentence rather than be answered as
# if it were whole.
pub class Request
  getter method : String
  getter path : String
  getter query : String?
  getter headers : Hash(String, String)
  getter version : String
  # The client's address, as the server's socket saw it (no port): nil
  # for a request a program made, or when the client had already gone. A
  # proxy in front is what this names; `X-Forwarded-For` is the proxy's
  # word, and a header any client can write.
  getter remote_address : String?
  # The client's certificate chain, leaf first, as std/tls checked it:
  # under `serve(tls:)` with `client_roots` set, the certificate the
  # client presented; empty otherwise.
  property peer_certificates : Array(Certificate) = [] of Certificate
  @reader : BodyReader
  @text : String?

  # A request whose body is in hand: what `parse_request` answers.
  def initialize(@method : String, @path : String, @query : String?, @headers : Hash(String, String), body : String, @version : String = "HTTP/1.1", @remote_address : String? = nil)
    @reader = BodyReader.text(body)
    @text = body
  end

  # A request whose body is still on the connection.
  def initialize(@method : String, @path : String, @query : String?, @headers : Hash(String, String), @version : String, @reader : BodyReader, @remote_address : String? = nil)
    @text = nil
  end

  def header(name : String) : String?
    @headers.each do |key, value|
      return value if HTTP.same_name?(key, name)
    end
    nil
  end

  def body_io : BodyReader
    @reader
  end

  def body : String
    if text = @text
      return text
    end
    got = @reader.read_all(Server::MAX_BODY.to_i64)
    raise got.message unless got.is_a?(String)
    @text = got
    got
  end

  # The pairs of the request's `Cookie` header, the first of a name kept.
  def cookies : Hash(String, String)
    found = header("Cookie")
    found ? HTTP.cookie_pairs(found) : {} of String => String
  end

  # The fields of a form: the query's, and an
  # `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` body's after them. The body is
  # read for it.
  def form : Params
    fields = Params.parse(@query || "")
    type = header("Content-Type")
    if type
      parsed = MIME.parse?(type)
      if parsed && parsed[0] == "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"
        Params.parse(body).each { |name, value| fields.add(name, value) }
      end
    end
    fields
  end

  # The body as `multipart/form-data` parts, read as they arrive; nil when
  # the `Content-Type` is not multipart or names no boundary
  # (`Multipart.boundary`).
  def multipart : Multipart?
    type = header("Content-Type")
    return nil unless type
    boundary = Multipart.boundary(type)
    return nil unless boundary
    Multipart.new(@text ? BodyReader.text(@text || "") : @reader, boundary)
  end
end

# What `HTTP.request`'s `jar:` keeps cookies in: the `Cookie` header a
# request to a URL carries, and an answer's `Set-Cookie` lines taken in.
# std/cookie's `CookieJar` is one; std/http itself reads no clock, so the
# expiry of a cookie is the store's to keep.
pub trait CookieStore
  abstract def header_for(url : String) : String?
  abstract def update(url : String, response : Response) : Nil
end

# A response: what a server's block answers, and what a client is handed.
#
# To write one: `Response.new(200, "hello")`, a status and a string;
# `Response.new(200, io)`, a stream the server copies out and closes, with
# the caller's `Content-Length` when it gives one and chunked otherwise;
# `Response.stream(200) { |w| w.write("...") }`, whose block writes as it
# goes; `Response.upgrade(headers) { |socket, leftover| ... }`, a 101 after
# which the connection is the block's. `add_header` writes a field on a
# line of its own, as each `Set-Cookie` must be.
#
# What a client is handed: the status, reason and headers, and the body -
# `body` whole, or `body_io` as it arrives inside `HTTP.open`'s block.
pub class Response
  getter status : Int32
  getter reason : String
  getter headers : Hash(String, String)
  # The values of each field on more than one line, by its name in
  # `headers`, one entry a line.
  @apart : Hash(String, Array(String))?
  @body : String
  @source : IyiIO?
  @writer : Proc(BodyWriter, Nil)?
  @takeover : Proc(IyiSocket | TlsStream, String, Nil)?
  @reader : BodyReader?
  @url : String?
  @decoded : Bool

  def initialize(@status : Int32, @reason : String, @headers : Hash(String, String), @body : String, @apart : Hash(String, Array(String))? = nil)
    @source = nil
    @writer = nil
    @takeover = nil
    @reader = nil
    @url = nil
    @decoded = false
  end

  # A response a client read: its body comes from *reader*, and *url* is
  # where it came from.
  def initialize(@status : Int32, @reason : String, @headers : Hash(String, String), @apart : Hash(String, Array(String))?, reader : BodyReader, url : String, @decoded : Bool)
    @body = ""
    @source = nil
    @writer = nil
    @takeover = nil
    @reader = reader
    @url = url
  end

  # A response to write: the reason is the status's own, the headers the
  # caller's. `Response.new(200, "hello")` is the common shape.
  def initialize(@status : Int32, @body : String = "", @headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String)
    @reason = HTTP.reason_for(@status)
    @apart = nil
    @source = nil
    @writer = nil
    @takeover = nil
    @reader = nil
    @url = nil
    @decoded = false
  end

  # A response whose body is *source*, copied out by the server and
  # closed after.
  def initialize(@status : Int32, source : IyiIO, @headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String)
    @reason = HTTP.reason_for(@status)
    @body = ""
    @apart = nil
    @source = source
    @writer = nil
    @takeover = nil
    @reader = nil
    @url = nil
    @decoded = false
  end

  # A response whose body the block writes as it goes, chunked.
  def self.stream(status : Int32, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, &writer : BodyWriter -> Nil) : Response
    response = Response.new(status, "", headers)
    response.writer = writer
    response
  end

  # A 101 Switching Protocols with *headers* (`Upgrade` and the
  # protocol's own), after which the connection is the block's: it is
  # handed the connection - the socket, or under `serve(tls:)` the
  # `TlsStream` over it - and the bytes the client sent after its request,
  # and the connection closes when it returns.
  def self.upgrade(headers : Hash(String, String), &takeover : (IyiSocket | TlsStream, String) -> Nil) : Response
    response = Response.new(101, "", headers)
    response.takeover = takeover
    response
  end

  protected def writer=(writer : Proc(BodyWriter, Nil)) : Proc(BodyWriter, Nil)
    @writer = writer
  end

  protected def takeover=(takeover : Proc(IyiSocket | TlsStream, String, Nil)) : Proc(IyiSocket | TlsStream, String, Nil)
    @takeover = takeover
  end

  def source : IyiIO?
    @source
  end

  def writer : Proc(BodyWriter, Nil)?
    @writer
  end

  def takeover : Proc(IyiSocket | TlsStream, String, Nil)?
    @takeover
  end

  # The URL the answer came from, after any redirects; nil for one a
  # program made.
  def url : String?
    @url
  end

  # Whether the client undid the body's `gzip`, `deflate` or `zstd`
  # coding; the `Content-Encoding` and `Content-Length` that described it
  # are gone from the headers then.
  def decoded? : Bool
    @decoded
  end

  # The body: whole. Inside `HTTP.open`'s block it is read here, the first
  # time it is asked for, and a body that cannot be read panics with why;
  # `body_io` answers that as a value.
  def body : String
    if reader = @reader
      got = reader.read_all
      raise got.message unless got.is_a?(String)
      @body = got
      @reader = nil
    end
    @body
  end

  # The body as it arrives. For a response a program made, its string.
  def body_io : BodyReader
    if reader = @reader
      return reader
    end
    BodyReader.text(@body)
  end

  # The body read whole into the response, which is answered; why not,
  # when it cannot be.
  def load : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    if reader = @reader
      got = reader.read_all
      return got unless got.is_a?(String)
      @body = got
      @reader = nil
    end
    self
  end

  # A header by name, as the server spelled it or otherwise: a field
  # name is case-insensitive (RFC 9110 §5.1), and `content-length` is as
  # common on the wire as `Content-Length`.
  def header(name : String) : String?
    @headers.each do |key, value|
      return value if HTTP.same_name?(key, name)
    end
    nil
  end

  # A field's lines apart, in the order they came: `header` joins them
  # with ", ", and `Set-Cookie` is the field that join breaks (RFC 9110
  # §5.3), since a cookie's `Expires` date has a comma in it - two cookies
  # read as "a=1; Expires=Wed, 21 Oct 2026 07:28:00 GMT, b=2", which no
  # split takes back apart. Empty for a field that is not there.
  def header_values(name : String) : Array(String)
    if apart = @apart
      apart.each do |key, values|
        return values.dup if HTTP.same_name?(key, name)
      end
    end
    found = header(name)
    found ? [found] : [] of String
  end

  # One more line of the field *name*, written on a line of its own: what
  # two `Set-Cookie`s need, since the ", " a field's lines are joined with
  # is part of a cookie's date.
  def add_header(name : String, value : String) : Nil
    spelled = name
    @headers.each do |key, _|
      spelled = key if HTTP.same_name?(key, name)
    end
    apart = @apart || {} of String => Array(String)
    @apart = apart
    if existing = @headers[spelled]?
      apart[spelled] = [existing] unless apart.has_key?(spelled)
      apart[spelled] << value
      @headers[spelled] = existing + ", " + value
    else
      @headers[spelled] = value
    end
  end

  # The lines of each field written on more than one, by its name in
  # `headers`; nil when every field is on one line.
  def apart : Hash(String, Array(String))?
    @apart
  end

  def success? : Bool
    @status >= 200 && @status < 300
  end
end

# The pieces of one request as they came off the wire, or why not: a
# request line and headers, the body they promise, and whether the
# client asked for the connection to close after. `consumed` is where it
# ends in the text it was read from; `status` the answer an error gets.
struct ParsedRequest
  getter request : Request?
  getter error : String?
  getter close : Bool
  getter consumed : Int32
  getter status : Int32

  def initialize(@request : Request?, @error : String?, @close : Bool, @consumed : Int32, @status : Int32 = 400)
  end
end

# A request's head, read and checked: everything but the body, and how
# the body is framed - `length` bytes, chunks, or none.
struct RequestHead
  getter method : String
  getter path : String
  getter query : String?
  getter headers : Hash(String, String)
  getter version : String
  getter close : Bool
  getter chunked : Bool
  getter length : Int64
  getter expects : Bool

  def initialize(@method : String, @path : String, @query : String?, @headers : Hash(String, String), @version : String, @close : Bool, @chunked : Bool, @length : Int64, @expects : Bool)
  end
end

pub struct Server
  # A request too large to be a request: 1 MiB of headers, 64 MiB of body.
  MAX_HEAD =  1048576
  MAX_BODY = 67108864
  # What a streaming block may leave unread and still have the connection
  # kept: the rest is read past up to this much, and past it the
  # connection is closed rather than read for as long as the client sends.
  MAX_DRAIN = 1048576
  # A string body shorter than this goes uncompressed: the gzip envelope
  # and the fixed code cost more than they save under it.
  COMPRESS_FROM = 1024
  # The defaults of `serve`'s *max_connections* and *request_timeout_ms*.
  MAX_CONNECTIONS =   1024
  REQUEST_TIMEOUT = 300000
  # How often an accept loop held at *max_connections* looks whether its
  # listener has been closed meanwhile.
  CAP_CHECK_MS = 250

  # Serve `listener` until it is closed. The block runs on the
  # connection's fiber, once per request; what it answers is written back.
  # The close ends a connection waiting on its client too, for its next
  # request or for the rest of one, and one that is answering ends when its
  # answer is written: a keep-alive client that sat idle kept `serve` from
  # returning for as long as it stayed, and so did one that had sent
  # what has arrived of a request so far - one byte of it was enough.
  #
  # *stream_bodies*: the block reads each request's body from `body_io`
  # rather than being handed it whole. *compress*: an answer whose type
  # std/mime calls compressible goes zstd or gzip to a client that
  # accepts one (`coding_for`).
  #
  # *timeout_ms*: a request's head must have arrived within it, counted
  # from when the server starts waiting for one - a kept-alive connection's
  # idle time included - and no read of a body nor write of an answer may
  # wait longer; a connection that does is closed. Without it, a client
  # that sends a byte of its head every few seconds (Slowloris) holds a
  # connection and its fiber for as long as it likes, and so does one that
  # reads none of its answer. A minute by default, nginx's
  # `client_header_timeout`; 0 for none. A connection handed over by
  # `Response.upgrade` waits as its protocol likes.
  #
  # *request_timeout_ms*: the whole request - head and body - must have
  # arrived within it, counted from the same moment as *timeout_ms*, as
  # Go's `ReadTimeout` beside its `ReadHeaderTimeout`; the head is held to
  # the sooner of the two. Without it a body sent a byte every few seconds
  # was bounded per read alone, and held its connection for as long as it
  # dripped. A block that reads the body itself (*stream_bodies*) reads
  # under it until the block returns, and what it leaves unread is read
  # past under it too. Over HTTP/2 the wait for each request and its body
  # is held to it on the connection the same way, the head to the sooner
  # of the two. Five minutes by default, which a 64 MiB body (`MAX_BODY`)
  # meets at 224 KB/s; a server taking large uploads over slow links
  # raises it. 0 for none. A connection past it is closed, unanswered, as
  # one past *timeout_ms* is.
  #
  # *max_connections*: at most this many connections are served at once;
  # 1024 by default, 0 for no limit. Past it the server stops accepting
  # until one ends, so the next connection waits in the kernel's listen
  # queue - queued, not refused, as Go's `netutil.LimitListener` does - and
  # is served in its turn; a client that gives up waiting goes unserved.
  # Each served connection holds its place until it closes, a kept-alive
  # one between requests too, which *timeout_ms* bounds. The process's
  # own limit on descriptors may come first.
  #
  # *tls*: each connection is TLS (std/tls's `TlsServer`, 1.3 or 1.2) under this
  # configuration, its handshake held to *timeout_ms* too; one whose
  # handshake fails is closed, the client told why by an alert. Inside
  # it a connection that opens with HTTP/2's preface - as a client whose
  # ALPN chose `h2` does, when `tls.alpn` offers it - is served HTTP/2,
  # and any other HTTP/1.1. A `Response.upgrade` block over TLS is handed
  # the `TlsStream`, so a WebSocket is `wss://`.
  def self.serve(listener : IyiSocket, stream_bodies : Bool = false, compress : Bool = false, timeout_ms : Int32 = 60000, tls : TlsServerConfig? = nil, request_timeout_ms : Int32 = REQUEST_TIMEOUT, max_connections : Int32 = MAX_CONNECTIONS, &handler : Request -> Response) : Nil
    raise "HTTP: a timeout of #{timeout_ms} ms" if timeout_ms < 0
    raise "HTTP: a request timeout of #{request_timeout_ms} ms" if request_timeout_ms < 0
    raise "HTTP: at most #{max_connections} connections" if max_connections < 0
    idle = {} of UInt64 => Conn
    # A place per connection served: a send waits while every one is held.
    slots = max_connections > 0 ? Channel(Int32).new(max_connections) : nil
    group do |g|
      pause = 5
      while !listener.closed?
        if room = slots
          break unless claim(room, listener)
        end
        case client = listener.accept
        in Cancelled
          slots.take_now if slots
          break
        in SocketError
          slots.take_now if slots
          # The listener's own trouble - descriptors run out, most often -
          # is waited out rather than spun on: 5 ms doubling to a second,
          # as Go's server does, and the next accept that works resets it.
          sleep(pause)
          pause = pause * 2 if pause < 1000
        in IyiSocket
          pause = 5
          spawn_handler(g, client, handler, listener, idle, stream_bodies, compress, timeout_ms, tls, request_timeout_ms, slots)
        end
      end
      # A copy: a stop's close_notify may wait, and other connections'
      # tasks leave the set meanwhile.
      idle.values.each { |waiting| waiting.close_for_stop }
      0
    end
  end

  # A place among *slots* for the next connection, waited for while all
  # are held: false when *listener* closes first, which is looked at every
  # `CAP_CHECK_MS` while waiting, since a close does not wake this wait.
  private def self.claim(slots : Channel(Int32), listener : IyiSocket) : Bool
    while !listener.closed?
      claimed = false
      select
      when slots.send(0)
        claimed = true
      when timeout(CAP_CHECK_MS)
        nil
      end
      return true if claimed
    end
    false
  end

  # The connection's task, spawned from a method so its block holds this
  # call's `client`. Spawned from the accept loop, the block held the loop's
  # own variable, which the next accept overwrote before the task first
  # ran: connections that arrived together were served by several tasks at
  # once - "two fibers reading one fd" - and the others by none, and their
  # clients waited forever.
  #
  # The connection runs in a group of its own whose answer is read, so a
  # block that panics ends its own connection - its defers close it, and the
  # panic is printed where it happened - and nothing else, which is what
  # Go's server does. Unread, the panic reached `serve`'s group, which
  # cancelled every other connection and the accept loop and panicked "a
  # task panicked": one request whose block failed took the server down.
  private def self.spawn_handler(g : IyiGroup, client : IyiSocket, handler : Proc(Request, Response), listener : IyiSocket, idle : Hash(UInt64, Conn), stream_bodies : Bool, compress : Bool, timeout_ms : Int32, tls : TlsServerConfig?, request_timeout_ms : Int32, slots : Channel(Int32)?) : Nil
    g.spawn do
      group do |own|
        served = own.spawn do
          handle(client, handler, listener, idle, stream_bodies, compress, timeout_ms, tls, request_timeout_ms)
          0
        end
        served.value
        0
      end
      # The connection's place, given back however it ended.
      slots.take_now if slots
      0
    end
  end

  # One connection: requests until the client is done. A request that is
  # not HTTP is answered with why and the connection closed, since the
  # bytes after it cannot be trusted to be a request either. Requests that
  # came in one read are read where they lie, one after another. A
  # connection that opens with HTTP/2's preface is served as HTTP/2. Under
  # *tls* the handshake comes first, and the connection ends with
  # close_notify.
  def self.handle(client : IyiSocket, handler : Proc(Request, Response), listener : IyiSocket? = nil, idle : Hash(UInt64, Conn)? = nil, stream_bodies : Bool = false, compress : Bool = false, timeout_ms : Int32 = 0, tls : TlsServerConfig? = nil, request_timeout_ms : Int32 = 0) : Nil
    defer client.close
    unless config = tls
      serve_connection(client, nil, handler, listener, idle, stream_bodies, compress, timeout_ms, request_timeout_ms)
      return
    end
    if timeout_ms > 0
      client.read_timeout_ms = timeout_ms
      client.write_timeout_ms = timeout_ms
    end
    # Early data is never taken (the header says why).
    secured = TlsServer.over(client, config, timeout_ms > 0 ? timeout_ms : TlsServer::HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT_MS, early_data: false)
    return if secured.is_a?(TlsError)
    serve_connection(client, TlsStream.new(secured, "the client"), handler, listener, idle, stream_bodies, compress, timeout_ms, request_timeout_ms)
    secured.close
  end

  # When a request whose wait began at *started* (monotonic ns) must have
  # arrived whole, 0 for no such time.
  private def self.until_whole(started : Int64, request_timeout_ms : Int32) : Int64
    request_timeout_ms > 0 ? started + request_timeout_ms.to_i64 * 1000000_i64 : 0_i64
  end

  # And its head: *timeout_ms* after *started*, or *whole* if sooner.
  private def self.until_head(started : Int64, timeout_ms : Int32, whole : Int64) : Int64
    head = timeout_ms > 0 ? started + timeout_ms.to_i64 * 1000000_i64 : 0_i64
    head = whole if whole > 0_i64 && (head == 0_i64 || whole < head)
    head
  end

  private def self.serve_connection(client : IyiSocket, stream : TlsStream?, handler : Proc(Request, Response), listener : IyiSocket?, idle : Hash(UInt64, Conn)?, stream_bodies : Bool, compress : Bool, timeout_ms : Int32, request_timeout_ms : Int32) : Nil
    conn = Conn.new(client, listener, idle, stream)
    conn.gap = timeout_ms if timeout_ms > 0
    remote = client.remote_address
    peer = stream ? stream.peer_certificates : [] of Certificate
    started = Time.monotonic_nanoseconds.to_i64
    conn.deadline_at(until_head(started, timeout_ms, until_whole(started, request_timeout_ms)))
    preface = h2_preface?(conn)
    conn.no_deadline
    if preface
      serve_h2(conn, handler, stream_bodies, compress, remote, peer, timeout_ms, request_timeout_ms)
      return
    end
    while true
      started = Time.monotonic_nanoseconds.to_i64
      whole = until_whole(started, request_timeout_ms)
      conn.deadline_at(until_head(started, timeout_ms, whole))
      got = conn.read_head(MAX_HEAD, "request", true)
      conn.no_deadline
      case got
      in Nil
        return
      in HTTPError
        refuse(conn.transport, got)
        linger(client)
        return
      in String
        parsed = parse_head(got)
        if parsed.is_a?(HTTPError)
          refuse(conn.transport, parsed)
          linger(client)
          return
        end
        head = parsed
        # The body, read here or by the block, is the request's too.
        conn.deadline_at(whole, "read a request") if whole > 0_i64
        reader = body_reader(conn, head)
        req = Request.new(head.method, head.path, head.query, head.headers, head.version, reader, remote)
        unless stream_bodies
          body = reader.read_all(MAX_BODY.to_i64)
          conn.no_deadline
          case body
          in String
            req = Request.new(head.method, head.path, head.query, head.headers, body, head.version, remote)
          in HTTPError
            # A client that went is not answered; one that sent a body that
            # is not one is told why.
            unless conn.ended?
              refuse(conn.transport, body)
              linger(client)
            end
            return
          in Cancelled
            return
          in SocketError
            return
          end
        end
        req.peer_certificates = peer
        response = handler.call(req)
        # Writing the answer waits as the client reads it, a pause at a time.
        conn.no_deadline
        return unless answered?(conn, response, req, head.close, compress)
        next if reader.finished?
        conn.deadline_at(whole, "read a request") if whole > 0_i64
        drained = reader.drain(MAX_DRAIN.to_i64)
        conn.no_deadline
        return unless drained
      end
    end
  end

  # Whether the connection opens with HTTP/2's preface, taken if it does:
  # read only as far as the bytes agree with it, so a request of HTTP/1.1
  # shorter than the preface is never waited on for more.
  private def self.h2_preface?(conn : Conn) : Bool
    wanted = H2Connection::PREFACE
    seen = 0
    while seen < wanted.bytesize
      return false if conn.available <= seen && !conn.fill
      while seen < conn.available && seen < wanted.bytesize
        return false if conn.peek(seen) != wanted.to_unsafe[seen]
        seen = seen + 1
      end
    end
    conn.consume(wanted.bytesize)
    true
  end

  # An HTTP/2 connection (RFC 9113): its requests, one stream at a time.
  # A body is read whole before the block runs unless *stream_bodies*,
  # as over HTTP/1.1; one past `MAX_BODY` is answered 413 on its stream.
  # The wait for the next request's head, and then for its body, are held
  # to *timeout_ms* and *request_timeout_ms* as over HTTP/1.1, counted
  # from when the wait began; the connection's every frame is read under
  # them meanwhile, so a peer that drips any stream's frames, or PINGs to
  # look alive, is cut off with the rest.
  private def self.serve_h2(conn : Conn, handler : Proc(Request, Response), stream_bodies : Bool, compress : Bool, remote : String?, peer : Array(Certificate), timeout_ms : Int32, request_timeout_ms : Int32) : Nil
    h2 = H2Connection.new(conn, true, MAX_BODY.to_i64)
    return unless h2.start
    while true
      started = Time.monotonic_nanoseconds.to_i64
      whole = until_whole(started, request_timeout_ms)
      conn.deadline_at(until_head(started, timeout_ms, whole))
      id = h2.next_request
      conn.no_deadline
      break unless id
      req = h2.request(id, remote)
      if req.nil?
        h2.done(id)
        next
      end
      conn.deadline_at(whole, "read a request") if whole > 0_i64
      unless stream_bodies
        body = req.body_io.read_all(MAX_BODY.to_i64)
        conn.no_deadline
        case body
        in String
          req = Request.new(req.method, req.path, req.query, req.headers, body, "HTTP/2", remote)
        in HTTPError
          h2.refuse_request(id, 413, body.reason) if body.status == 413
          h2.done(id)
          next
        in Cancelled
          break
        in SocketError
          break
        end
      end
      req.peer_certificates = peer
      response = handler.call(req)
      conn.no_deadline
      answered = h2.answer(id, response, req, compress)
      h2.done(id)
      break unless answered
    end
    h2.goaway
    linger(conn.socket) if h2.failure.is_a?(HTTPError)
  end

  LINGER_BYTES = 65536
  LINGER_MS    =  1000

  # What a refused client goes on sending, read and dropped until it
  # closes - at most `LINGER_BYTES` and `LINGER_MS` in all - before the
  # close: a close with its bytes unread is a reset, which can take the
  # refusal (a 4xx answer, GOAWAY) from the client's buffer before it is
  # read, as macOS's does.
  private def self.linger(socket : IyiSocket) : Nil
    return if socket.closed?
    until_ns = Time.monotonic_nanoseconds.to_i64 + LINGER_MS.to_i64 * 1000000_i64
    left = LINGER_BYTES
    while left > 0
      wait = (until_ns - Time.monotonic_nanoseconds.to_i64) // 1000000_i64
      break if wait <= 0
      socket.read_timeout_ms = wait.to_i32
      got = socket.read(left < 16384 ? left : 16384)
      break unless got.is_a?(String) && !got.empty?
      left = left - got.bytesize
    end
  end

  # The body *head* promises, read from *conn*: under the server's limit,
  # and with `Expect: 100-continue` answered the first time the body is
  # waited for - not before it is wanted, and not when it has already
  # come. A client told nothing holds its body back, and curl waits a
  # second before every upload past a megabyte.
  private def self.body_reader(conn : Conn, head : RequestHead) : BodyReader
    hello = nil.as(Proc(Bool)?)
    if head.expects
      socket = conn.transport
      hello = -> { socket.write("HTTP/1.1 100 Continue\r\n\r\n").is_a?(Int32) }
    end
    if head.chunked
      BodyReader.new(conn, BodyReader::CHUNKED, 0_i64, MAX_BODY.to_i64, "", hello)
    elsif head.length > 0_i64
      BodyReader.new(conn, BodyReader::LENGTH, head.length, MAX_BODY.to_i64, "", hello)
    else
      BodyReader.new(conn, BodyReader::EMPTY, 0_i64, MAX_BODY.to_i64)
    end
  end

  private def self.refuse(client : IyiSocket | TlsStream, error : HTTPError) : Nil
    client.write(format_response(Response.new(error.status, error.reason + "\n"), false, true))
  end

  # The block's answer to *req*, written: whether the connection goes on.
  # A client that went away before its answer is gone; its connection ends
  # here, and the server goes on. An answer whose own `Connection` says
  # `close` closes the connection after it, as Go's server and Python's
  # do: the line was dropped, `keep-alive` written in its place, and the
  # next request served on a connection the block had ended.
  private def self.answered?(conn : Conn, response : Response, req : Request, close : Bool, compress : Bool) : Bool
    closing = close
    if said = response.header("Connection")
      closing = true if HTTP.has_token?(said, "close")
    end
    socket = conn.transport
    head = HTTP.same_name?(req.method, "HEAD")
    if takeover = response.takeover
      return false unless socket.write(format_head(response, false, false, nil, false, true)).is_a?(Int32)
      leftover = conn.leftover
      conn.unlimited
      conn.held { takeover.call(socket, leftover) }
      return false
    end
    coding = compress ? Server.coding_for(req, response) : nil
    if source = response.source
      defer source.close
      return send_source(socket, response, source, head, closing, req.version == "HTTP/1.0", !coding.nil?)
    end
    if writer = response.writer
      return send_stream(socket, response, writer, head, closing, req.version == "HTTP/1.0", !coding.nil?)
    end
    response = Server.compressed(response, coding) if coding && response.body.bytesize >= COMPRESS_FROM
    socket.write(format_response(response, head, closing)).is_a?(Int32) && !closing
  end

  # A stream copied out: *length* bytes of it when the block gave a
  # `Content-Length`, which closes the connection if the stream has fewer;
  # chunked otherwise, or to the close for an HTTP/1.0 client, which reads
  # no chunks.
  private def self.send_source(socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream, response : Response, source : IyiIO, head : Bool, close : Bool, old : Bool, gzip : Bool) : Bool
    length = nil.as(Int64?)
    if declared = response.header("Content-Length")
      n = HTTP.content_length(declared)
      raise "HTTP: Content-Length #{declared.inspect} is not a number" unless n
      length = n
    end
    gzip = false if length
    bodiless = HTTP.bodiless?(response.status)
    chunked = length.nil? && !old && !head && !bodiless
    closing = close || (length.nil? && !chunked && !head && !bodiless)
    response = Server.coded(response, "gzip") if gzip
    return false unless socket.write(format_head(response, head, closing, length, chunked)).is_a?(Int32)
    return !closing if head || bodiless
    if want = length
      left = want
      while left > 0_i64
        piece = source.read_bytes(left > 65536_i64 ? 65536 : left.to_i32)
        return false if piece.bytesize == 0
        return false unless socket.write(piece).is_a?(Int32)
        left = left - piece.bytesize.to_i64
      end
      return !closing
    end
    writer = Server.writer_for(socket, chunked, gzip)
    while true
      piece = source.read_bytes(65536)
      break if piece.bytesize == 0
      return false unless writer.write(piece).nil?
    end
    writer.finish.nil? && !closing
  end

  # A body the block writes as it goes: chunked, or to the close for an
  # HTTP/1.0 client. A HEAD is answered without running the block.
  private def self.send_stream(socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream, response : Response, writer_block : Proc(BodyWriter, Nil), head : Bool, close : Bool, old : Bool, gzip : Bool) : Bool
    bodiless = HTTP.bodiless?(response.status)
    chunked = !old && !head && !bodiless
    closing = close || (!chunked && !head && !bodiless)
    response = Server.coded(response, "gzip") if gzip
    return false unless socket.write(format_head(response, head, closing, nil, chunked)).is_a?(Int32)
    return !closing if head || bodiless
    writer = Server.writer_for(socket, chunked, gzip)
    writer_block.call(writer)
    writer.finish.nil? && !closing
  end

  # The coding *response* goes in to the client of *req*, or nil: one the
  # client takes (RFC 9110 §12.5.3, a `q` of 0 refusing it), for an
  # answer with a body and a type worth compressing, and no coding of its
  # own. A whole body goes `zstd` when the client's `q` for it is at least
  # its `q` for gzip, and `gzip` otherwise. The tie goes to zstd for the
  # server's time: measured on this machine, release builds, on
  # src/std/http.iyi (199,134 bytes) `Gzip.compress` wrote 54,484 bytes in
  # 5 ms and `Zstd.compress` 61,753 in 1 ms, and on CHANGELOG.md (1.5 MB)
  # 552,446 in 58 ms against 589,785 in 13 ms - a quarter of the time, for
  # an eighth more bytes at most. A stream - an `IyiIO` or a
  # `Response.stream` - goes gzip or as it is: std/compress's `Deflater`
  # writes each piece as it comes, where std/zstd's compressor takes its
  # input whole, and a frame a write would code each piece alone.
  def self.coding_for(req : Request, response : Response) : String?
    return nil if response.header("Content-Encoding")
    status = response.status
    return nil if status < 200 || HTTP.bodiless?(status) || status == 206
    type = response.header("Content-Type")
    return nil unless type && MIME.compressible?(type)
    accepted = req.header("Accept-Encoding")
    return nil unless accepted
    gzip = HTTP.coding_weight(accepted, "gzip")
    if response.source.nil? && response.writer.nil?
      zstd = HTTP.coding_weight(accepted, "zstd")
      return "zstd" if zstd > 0 && zstd >= gzip
    end
    gzip > 0 ? "gzip" : nil
  end

  # The headers a coded body goes with: the coding, and `Vary` so that a
  # cache keeps the answers apart. A `Content-Length` the block gave is
  # the plain body's, and goes.
  protected def self.coded(response : Response, coding : String) : Response
    headers = {} of String => String
    response.headers.each do |name, value|
      headers[name] = value unless HTTP.same_name?(name, "Content-Length")
    end
    headers["Content-Encoding"] = coding
    vary = response.header("Vary")
    if vary.nil?
      headers["Vary"] = "Accept-Encoding"
    elsif !HTTP.has_token?(vary, "Accept-Encoding")
      named = "Vary"
      headers.each { |name, _| named = name if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Vary") }
      headers[named] = vary + ", Accept-Encoding"
    end
    coded = Response.new(response.status, response.reason, headers, response.body, response.apart)
    coded
  end

  # A string body compressed whole in *coding*, `zstd` or `gzip`, with
  # the headers that say so. `Zstd.compress`'s frame needs a window of
  # 1 MiB at most, inside the 8 MB RFC 9659 §3 holds an encoder to.
  protected def self.compressed(response : Response, coding : String) : Response
    coded = Server.coded(response, coding)
    body = coding == "zstd" ? Zstd.compress(response.body) : HTTP.gzip(response.body)
    Response.new(coded.status, coded.reason, coded.headers, body, coded.apart)
  end

  protected def self.writer_for(socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream, chunked : Bool, gzip : Bool) : BodyWriter
    gzip ? HTTP.gzip_writer(socket, chunked) : BodyWriter.new(socket, chunked)
  end

  # A request at *from* in `text`, if a whole one is there. No request and
  # no error means "not yet". `consumed` counts from the start of `text`.
  def self.parse_request(text : String, from : Int32 = 0) : ParsedRequest
    from = HTTP.past_empty_lines(text, from)
    split_at = HTTP.find_headers_end(text, from)
    unless split_at
      return ParsedRequest.new(nil, "request headers past #{MAX_HEAD} bytes", true, 0, 431) if text.bytesize - from > MAX_HEAD
      return ParsedRequest.new(nil, nil, false, 0)
    end
    parsed = parse_head(text[from, split_at - from])
    return ParsedRequest.new(nil, parsed.reason, true, 0, parsed.status) if parsed.is_a?(HTTPError)
    head = parsed
    body_start = split_at + 4
    if head.chunked
      # Its end is looked for where it lies in what has arrived.
      case done = HTTP.chunked_end(text, body_start)
      in Nil
        return ParsedRequest.new(nil, nil, head.close, 0)
      in String
        return ParsedRequest.new(nil, "chunked body: #{done}", true, 0)
      in Int32
        return ParsedRequest.new(nil, "chunked body past #{MAX_BODY} bytes", true, 0, 413) if done - body_start > MAX_BODY
        body = HTTP.decode_chunked(text[body_start, done - body_start])
        return ParsedRequest.new(Request.new(head.method, head.path, head.query, head.headers, body), nil, head.close, done)
      end
    end
    length = head.length.to_i32
    return ParsedRequest.new(nil, nil, false, 0) if text.bytesize - body_start < length
    ParsedRequest.new(Request.new(head.method, head.path, head.query, head.headers, text[body_start, length]), nil, head.close, body_start + length)
  end

  # A request's head - the text before its blank line - read and checked,
  # or why it is not one.
  def self.parse_head(text : String) : RequestHead | HTTPError
    lines = HTTP.split_crlf(text)
    return HTTPError.new("empty request") if lines.size == 0
    first = lines[0]
    sp1 = first.index(' ')
    return HTTPError.new("not a request line: #{first.inspect}") unless sp1
    rest = first[sp1 + 1, first.bytesize - sp1 - 1]
    sp2 = rest.index(' ')
    return HTTPError.new("not a request line: #{first.inspect}") unless sp2
    method = first[0, sp1]
    target = rest[0, sp2]
    version = rest[sp2 + 1, rest.bytesize - sp2 - 1]
    return HTTPError.new("not a method: #{method.inspect}") unless HTTP.token?(method)
    return HTTPError.new("not an HTTP/1 request: #{version.inspect}") unless version == "HTTP/1.1" || version == "HTTP/1.0"
    # A control byte in the target - a bare CR, a NUL - is not part of any
    # URI (RFC 9112 §2.2, §3.2); passed on, a query with a line break in it
    # broke the answer of a block that echoed it into a header.
    return HTTPError.new("not a request target: #{target.inspect}") if target.bytesize == 0 || HTTP.control?(target)
    headers = case fields = HTTP.parse_fields(lines)
              in String
                return HTTPError.new(fields)
              in Hash(String, String)
                fields
              end
    # Host (RFC 9112 §3.2): an HTTP/1.1 request names one, no request names
    # two, and what it names is a host; a server answers 400 otherwise. This
    # one served a request with no Host, and handed the block two as one,
    # `Host: a` and `Host: b` read as "a, b".
    hosts = 0
    lines.each do |line|
      hosts = hosts + 1 if line.bytesize > 4 && line.to_unsafe[4] == 58_u8 && HTTP.same_name?(line[0, 4], "Host")
    end
    return HTTPError.new("Host on #{hosts} lines") if hosts > 1
    return HTTPError.new("an HTTP/1.1 request without Host") if hosts == 0 && version == "HTTP/1.1"
    host_name = "Host"
    headers.each do |name, value|
      next unless HTTP.same_name?(name, "Host")
      return HTTPError.new("not a host: #{value.inspect}") unless HTTP.host?(value)
      host_name = name
    end
    # The absolute form (RFC 9112 §3.2.2), which a server must accept,
    # names the path after the authority: `http://h/x?q` is path `/x` and
    # query `q`, as the other library reads it, where the block was handed
    # `http://h/x` as the path.
    origin = target
    if authority = HTTP.absolute_form(target)
      p = target.to_unsafe
      after_authority = authority
      while after_authority < target.bytesize && p[after_authority] != 47_u8 && p[after_authority] != 63_u8
        after_authority = after_authority + 1
      end
      # And its authority is the host, whatever the Host line says: `GET
      # http://b/` under `Host: a` asks b. The block reads it as the Host;
      # it was handed the line's, and had no way to the target's.
      named = target[authority, after_authority - authority]
      return HTTPError.new("not a host: #{named.inspect}") unless named.bytesize > 0 && HTTP.host?(named)
      headers[host_name] = named
      origin = target[after_authority, target.bytesize - after_authority]
      origin = "/" + origin if origin.bytesize == 0 || origin.starts_with?("?")
    end
    path = origin
    query = nil.as(String?)
    if q = origin.index('?')
      path = origin[0, q]
      query = origin[q + 1, origin.bytesize - q - 1]
    end
    stub = Request.new(method, path, query, headers, "")
    # `Connection` is a list of options (RFC 9110 §7.6.1), and `close`
    # among them closes (RFC 9112 §9.6): read as one value, `close, TE` and
    # two `Connection: close` lines, joined to `close, close`, kept the
    # connection open and were answered `keep-alive`.
    close = version == "HTTP/1.0"
    if conn = stub.header("Connection")
      close = HTTP.has_token?(conn, "close") || (version == "HTTP/1.0" && !HTTP.has_token?(conn, "keep-alive"))
    end
    expects = version == "HTTP/1.1" && HTTP.same_name?(HTTP.trim(stub.header("Expect") || ""), "100-continue")
    if te = stub.header("Transfer-Encoding")
      return HTTPError.new("transfer encoding #{te.inspect} is not chunked") unless HTTP.same_name?(HTTP.trim(te), "chunked")
      # A length beside the chunks, or chunks in HTTP/1.0, is framing that
      # a reader ahead of this one may have taken otherwise (RFC 9112 §6.1,
      # §6.3): the connection closes after the answer, where what followed
      # was read as the next request.
      close = true if stub.header("Content-Length") || version == "HTTP/1.0"
      return RequestHead.new(method, path, query, headers, version, close, true, 0_i64, expects)
    end
    length = 0_i64
    if cl = stub.header("Content-Length")
      n = HTTP.content_length(cl)
      return HTTPError.new("Content-Length is not a number: #{cl.inspect}") unless n
      # A length past 2147483647 is one too, read as one past it: it was
      # answered "not a number", where it is a body too large.
      return HTTPError.new("body past #{MAX_BODY} bytes", 413) if n > MAX_BODY.to_i64
      length = n
    end
    RequestHead.new(method, path, query, headers, version, close, false, length, expects)
  end

  # The response as it goes on the wire, whole: `format_head` for its
  # string body, and the body.
  def self.format_response(response : Response, head : Bool, close : Bool) : String
    text = format_head(response, head, close, response.body.bytesize.to_i64, false)
    head || HTTP.bodiless?(response.status) ? text : text + response.body
  end

  # A response's head. `Content-Length` is *length*'s - the caller's own
  # line is checked against it, and kept only where no body follows it: a
  # HEAD, or a 304 that names the length a 200 would have (RFC 9110 §8.6) -
  # or `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` when *chunked*, or neither for a body
  # the connection's close ends. `Connection` says what the connection will
  # do next, except on an *upgrade*, whose own line says it. A 1xx, 204 or
  # 304 has no content (RFC 9110 §15), and writes neither a body nor a
  # length of its own (§8.6): the body was read by the client as the start
  # of the next answer, and `Content-Length: 0` on a 304 told a cache that
  # the resource had become empty. A field added on lines of its own
  # (`add_header`) is written a line each.
  def self.format_head(response : Response, head : Bool, close : Bool, length : Int64?, chunked : Bool, upgrade : Bool = false) : String
    # A status line the client half reads as written, refused otherwise as
    # a request's lines are: `Response.new(200, "OK\r\nSet-Cookie: a=1",
    # ...)` went out with a header of its own in the reason, and 2000 as
    # `HTTP/1.1 2000 Server Error`, which `parse_response` refuses.
    raise "HTTP: not a status: #{response.status}" unless response.status >= 100 && response.status <= 999
    raise "HTTP: the reason contains a line break" if HTTP.has_break?(response.reason)
    raise "HTTP: the reason contains a control character" if HTTP.control?(response.reason)
    text = String::Builder.new
    text << "HTTP/1.1 " << response.status << " " << response.reason << "\r\n"
    has_length = false
    apart = response.apart
    response.headers.each do |name, value|
      raise "HTTP: not a header name: #{name.inspect}" unless HTTP.token?(name)
      lines = nil.as(Array(String)?)
      if split = apart
        lines = split[name]?
      end
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a line break" if HTTP.has_break?(value)
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a control character" if HTTP.control?(value)
      next if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Connection") && !upgrade
      # The body goes with its length or its chunks, which are the server's
      # to write: a caller's chunked framing went out beside
      # `Content-Length` (RFC 9112 §6.1), and a caller's length was written
      # whatever the body was, so "2" on "hello" was read as "he" and "llo"
      # began the next answer.
      raise "HTTP: #{name} is not written; the body goes with its length" if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Transfer-Encoding")
      if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Content-Length")
        unless head || response.status == 304 || length.nil?
          n = HTTP.content_length(value)
          raise "HTTP: #{name} #{value.inspect} is not the body's #{length} bytes" unless n && n == length
        end
        has_length = true
      end
      if each_line = lines
        each_line.each { |line| text << name << ": " << line << "\r\n" }
      else
        text << name << ": " << value << "\r\n"
      end
    end
    unless has_length || HTTP.bodiless?(response.status)
      if chunked
        text << "Transfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n"
      elsif written = length
        text << "Content-Length: " << written << "\r\n"
      end
    end
    text << (close ? "Connection: close\r\n" : "Connection: keep-alive\r\n") unless upgrade
    text << "\r\n"
    text.to_s
  end
end

# A request on its way: the connection, and the answer whose head has been
# read. `close` ends it; inside `HTTP.open` that is done for the caller.
class Exchange
  getter conn : Conn
  getter response : Response
  # The client the connection goes back to, and whether it may: the
  # answer was HTTP/1.1, did not say `close`, and is framed by a length
  # or chunks rather than by the connection's end.
  @client : HTTPClient?
  @key : String
  @keep : Bool

  # The body as it came off the connection: the response's own `body_io`
  # is a string once `load` has read it.
  @reader : BodyReader
  # The HTTP/2 stream the answer came on, 0 over HTTP/1.1.
  property h2_id : Int32 = 0

  def initialize(@conn : Conn, @response : Response, @client : HTTPClient? = nil, @key : String = "", @keep : Bool = false)
    @reader = @response.body_io
  end

  # The connection closed, or handed back to its client once the body has
  # been read to its end - what the caller left of it read past, up to
  # 64 KiB, and the connection closed past that, as Go's client does. An
  # HTTP/2 answer left unread past that is cancelled on its stream alone,
  # and the connection goes back while it can take another request.
  def close : Nil
    client = @client
    if h2 = @conn.h2
      @reader.drain(65536_i64)
      h2.forget(@h2_id)
      if client && h2.usable?
        client.give_back(@key, @conn)
      else
        @conn.close
      end
      return
    end
    if client && @keep && !@conn.ended? && @reader.drain(65536_i64) && @conn.available == 0
      client.give_back(@key, @conn)
    else
      @conn.close
    end
  end
end

# Requests that keep their connections: an `HTTPClient` holds the connections
# its answers left open, by host and port, and the next request to that
# server goes over one of them instead of a new one - the TCP handshake,
# and the slow start after it, paid once rather than per request. Its
# methods are `HTTP`'s, redirects, cookies and decoding included:
#
# ```
# client = HTTPClient.new
# client.get("http://127.0.0.1:8080/a")
# client.get("http://127.0.0.1:8080/b") # the same connection
# client.close
# ```
#
# A connection goes back once its answer has been read to the end (inside
# `open`, up to 64 KiB left unread is read past for it) and the answer
# allows it; at most *idle* wait per server, and the rest close. A server
# may close an idle connection whenever it likes (RFC 9112 §9.3.1), so a
# request whose kept connection turns out closed - the write fails, or it
# ends before a byte of an answer - is sent again once on a new one, when
# its method is idempotent (RFC 9110 §9.2.2) and its body a string;
# anything else answers the failure. One task at a time per request: two
# tasks sending at once take two connections, and a client is not for
# sharing across threads. An `https://` connection is kept under the
# `HTTP.tls_config` it was made with, and handed only to a request made
# under the same one (`TlsMadeUnder`): after `HTTP.tls_config=` the next
# request makes a connection of its own, checked against the new roots.
#
# Over HTTP/2 - `https://` where ALPN chose `h2`, or `http://` with
# *prior_knowledge* (h2c, RFC 9113 §3.3) - a kept connection carries the
# next request on a new stream, until the server says GOAWAY.
pub class HTTPClient
  @idle : Hash(String, Array(Conn))
  @limit : Int32
  @timeout_ms : Int32
  @made : Int32
  @reused : Int32
  # `http://` spoken as HTTP/2 from the first byte, without asking: for a
  # server known to take it.
  getter prior_knowledge : Bool
  # The proxy every request goes through, `HTTP.open`'s *proxy*; its
  # connections are kept under it.
  getter proxy : String?

  # *timeout_ms* and *proxy* are `HTTP.open`'s, for every request the
  # client sends.
  def initialize(idle : Int32 = 4, @timeout_ms : Int32 = 0, @prior_knowledge : Bool = false, @proxy : String? = nil)
    raise "HTTP: a client keeps 0 or more idle connections, not #{idle}" if idle < 0
    raise "HTTP: a timeout of #{@timeout_ms} ms" if @timeout_ms < 0
    @idle = {} of String => Array(Conn)
    @limit = idle
    @made = 0
    @reused = 0
  end

  # Connections this client has opened, and requests sent over one it
  # kept: what a program reads to see that its connections are kept.
  def connections_made : Int32
    @made
  end

  def connections_reused : Int32
    @reused
  end

  def request(method : String, url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, body : String | IyiIO = "", redirects : Int32 = 10, jar : CookieStore? = nil, decompress : Bool = true) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    HTTP.open(method, url, headers, body, redirects, jar, decompress, self, @timeout_ms, @proxy) { |response| response.load }
  end

  def open(method : String, url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, body : String | IyiIO = "", redirects : Int32 = 10, jar : CookieStore? = nil, decompress : Bool = true, &block : Response -> R) : R | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError forall R
    HTTP.open(method, url, headers, body, redirects, jar, decompress, self, @timeout_ms, @proxy) { |response| block.call(response) }
  end

  def get(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("GET", url, headers)
  end

  def head(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("HEAD", url, headers)
  end

  def post(url : String, body : String | IyiIO = "", headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("POST", url, headers, body)
  end

  def put(url : String, body : String | IyiIO = "", headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("PUT", url, headers, body)
  end

  def delete(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("DELETE", url, headers)
  end

  # Every idle connection closed. The client may be used after; it opens
  # new ones.
  def close : Nil
    @idle.each do |_, conns|
      conns.each { |conn| conn.close }
    end
    @idle = {} of String => Array(Conn)
  end

  # A kept connection to *key*, nil when none waits. An HTTP/2 connection
  # that can take no more requests is closed rather than handed out, and
  # so is an `https://` one made under another TLS configuration than
  # *tls*, the one the request is made under (`TlsMadeUnder`): keyed by
  # scheme, host and port alone, a connection whose server was checked
  # against one set of roots carried requests after `HTTP.tls_config=`
  # named others that would have refused it.
  protected def take(key : String, tls : TlsConfig? = nil) : Conn?
    if waiting = @idle[key]?
      while conn = waiting.pop?
        h2 = conn.h2
        if h2 && !h2.usable?
          conn.close
          next
        end
        under = conn.made_under
        if tls && !(under && under.same?(tls))
          conn.close
          next
        end
        @reused = @reused + 1
        return conn
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  protected def made : Nil
    @made = @made + 1
  end

  protected def give_back(key : String, conn : Conn) : Nil
    waiting = @idle[key]? || [] of Conn
    if waiting.size >= @limit
      conn.close
      return
    end
    waiting << conn
    @idle[key] = waiting
  end
end

# What `HTTP.upgrade` answers: the 101, and the connection, which is the
# caller's now - the socket, and the bytes the server sent after its head.
pub class Upgraded
  getter response : Response
  # The connection: the socket, or for `https://` the `TlsStream` over it.
  getter socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream
  getter leftover : String

  def initialize(@response : Response, @socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream, @leftover : String)
  end
end

# A proxy a request goes through, read from its URL once per `open`: its
# host and port, the `Proxy-Authorization` its user and password make
# (Basic, RFC 7617, the user and password decoded first, as curl and Go
# send them), and the text a kept connection is filed under - the URL
# whole, credentials included, so two users of one proxy do not share a
# connection that the proxy may have tied to the first. A URL with no
# scheme is `http://`'s, as Go and curl read `HTTP_PROXY=host:3128`; the
# port is 80 when none is written, as Go's (curl's is 1080). Only an
# `http://` proxy is spoken: TLS to the proxy itself (`https://`) and
# SOCKS are refused by name rather than spoken in the clear.
struct ProxyRoute
  getter host : String
  getter port : Int32
  getter authorization : String?
  getter key : String

  def initialize(@host : String, @port : Int32, @authorization : String?, @key : String)
  end

  # The proxy as a message names it: host and port, never the password.
  def label : String
    (@host.includes?(":") ? "[#{@host}]" : @host) + ":" + @port.to_s
  end

  def self.parse(text : String) : ProxyRoute | HTTPError
    written = text.includes?("://") ? text : "http://" + text
    # The URL is not repeated in the sentence: it may hold a password.
    uri = URI.parse?(written)
    return HTTPError.new("the proxy is not a URL") unless uri
    scheme = (uri.scheme || "").downcase
    return HTTPError.new("a proxy over #{scheme}://, which this client does not speak; only http:// proxies") unless scheme == "http"
    host = uri.hostname
    return HTTPError.new("the proxy's URL names no host") unless host && host.bytesize > 0
    credential = nil.as(String?)
    if user = uri.user
      credential = "Basic " + Base64.encode(user + ":" + (uri.password || ""))
    end
    ProxyRoute.new(host, uri.port || 80, credential, "proxy " + written)
  end

  # The route *proxy* names, nil for none.
  def self.of(proxy : String?) : ProxyRoute | HTTPError | Nil
    return nil unless proxy
    parse(proxy)
  end
end

pub struct HTTP
  @@tls : TlsConfig? = nil

  # What an `https://` request is checked against and offers: the
  # system's roots, and ALPN's `http/1.1`, unless a program sets its own -
  # roots of its own for a server it runs, say.
  def self.tls_config : TlsConfig
    if held = @@tls
      return held
    end
    config = TlsConfig.new
    config.alpn = ["http/1.1"]
    @@tls = config
    config
  end

  def self.tls_config=(config : TlsConfig) : TlsConfig
    @@tls = config
  end

  # The request as it goes on the wire, whole. `Host`, `Connection` and
  # `Content-Length` are the request's own lines; a caller's header of
  # that name is refused rather than sent twice. A body's length is
  # always written for the methods a body belongs to, so `POST` with
  # nothing to say still says `Content-Length: 0`. A byte of the path past
  # 127 goes as `%` and two hex digits: a request target is ASCII (RFC
  # 9112 §3.2, RFC 3986 §2.1), and `/ü` went out as its UTF-8 bytes, raw.
  def self.format_request(method : String, path : String, host : String, body : String = "", headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : String
    length = body.bytesize > 0 || same_name?(method, "POST") || same_name?(method, "PUT") || same_name?(method, "PATCH") ? body.bytesize.to_i64 : nil
    request_head(method, path, host, headers, length, false, "close") + body
  end

  # A request's head: the request line, `Host`, `Connection` (*connection*:
  # `close`, or `Upgrade`), the caller's headers, and the body's framing -
  # *length*, chunks, or none.
  def self.request_head(method : String, path : String, host : String, headers : Hash(String, String), length : Int64?, chunked : Bool, connection : String) : String
    raise "HTTP: not a method: #{method.inspect}" unless token?(method)
    raise "HTTP: not a request path: #{path.inspect}" if path.bytesize == 0 || has_space_or_control?(path)
    raise "HTTP: not a host: #{host.inspect}" if host.bytesize == 0 || has_space_or_control?(host)
    req = String::Builder.new
    req << method << " " << ascii_target(path) << " HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: " << host << "\r\nConnection: " << connection << "\r\n"
    headers.each do |name, value|
      raise "HTTP: not a header name: #{name.inspect}" unless token?(name)
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a line break" if has_break?(value)
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a control character" if control?(value)
      if same_name?(name, "Host") || same_name?(name, "Connection") || same_name?(name, "Content-Length")
        raise "HTTP: #{name} is the request's own header"
      end
      # A request's body goes with its length or its chunks, which are the
      # request's to write: a caller's `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` was
      # written beside `Content-Length`, a pair RFC 9112 §6.2 forbids,
      # over a body that was not chunked, and the module's own server,
      # which reads the chunks first, took "abc" for an unfinished chunk
      # size and waited for the rest.
      raise "HTTP: #{name} is not sent; a request's body goes with its length" if same_name?(name, "Transfer-Encoding")
      req << name << ": " << value << "\r\n"
    end
    if chunked
      req << "Transfer-Encoding: chunked\r\n"
    elsif sent = length
      req << "Content-Length: " << sent << "\r\n"
    end
    req << "\r\n"
    req.to_s
  end

  # The response as it came off the wire, whole. `head` says the request
  # was a `HEAD`, whose answer carries the headers of a body it does not
  # send. An interim answer - `100 Continue`, `103 Early Hints` - is read
  # past to the final one after it (RFC 9110 §15.2). A `101` switches
  # protocols, and HTTP ends with it. What is not a response panics with
  # why: this is the text a program has in hand; the client answers the
  # same sentences as `HTTPError`s.
  def self.parse_response(text : String, head : Bool = false) : Response
    start = 0
    status = 0
    reason = ""
    headers = {} of String => String
    apart = nil.as(Hash(String, Array(String))?)
    while true
      split_at = find_headers_end(text, start)
      raise "HTTP: no header terminator" unless split_at
      parsed = parse_response_head(text[start, split_at - start])
      raise parsed.message if parsed.is_a?(HTTPError)
      status, reason, headers, apart = parsed
      start = split_at + 4
      break unless status >= 100 && status < 200 && status != 101 && start < text.bytesize
    end
    body = text[start, text.bytesize - start]
    return Response.new(status, reason, headers, "", apart) if head || bodiless?(status)
    if te = header_in(headers, "Transfer-Encoding")
      # Chunked, once and last, is the coding this client decodes (RFC 9112
      # §6.3): `gzip, chunked` and two `chunked` lines were taken for no
      # coding at all, and the chunks came back as the body of a 200,
      # framing and all.
      if refused = coding_refused(te)
        raise "HTTP: " + refused
      end
      body = decode_chunked(body)
    elsif cl = header_in(headers, "Content-Length")
      n = content_length(cl)
      raise "HTTP: Content-Length is not a number: #{cl.inspect}" unless n
      raise "HTTP: Content-Length #{cl.inspect} is past the 2147483647 bytes a string holds" if n > 2147483647_i64
      length = n.to_i32
      # Short of it is a connection that ended early, as a chunked body cut
      # inside a chunk is: it was answered as a whole 200 with the part
      # that came, a cut-off download read as a good one.
      raise "HTTP: the body ended after #{body.bytesize} of its #{length} bytes" if body.bytesize < length
      body = body[0, length] if length < body.bytesize
    end
    Response.new(status, reason, headers, body, apart)
  end

  # A response's head - the text before its blank line - read: status,
  # reason, the fields, and the lines of those on more than one. A line
  # that begins with a blank continues the field above it (obs-fold, RFC
  # 9112 §5.2), which a user agent joins on with one space.
  def self.parse_response_head(text : String) : {Int32, String, Hash(String, String), Hash(String, Array(String))?} | HTTPError
    lines = split_crlf(text)
    return HTTPError.new("empty response") if lines.size == 0
    line = status_line(lines[0])
    return line if line.is_a?(HTTPError)
    status, reason = line
    lines_of = {} of String => Array(String)
    headers = case fields = parse_fields(lines, unfold: true, apart: lines_of)
              in String
                return HTTPError.new(fields)
              in Hash(String, String)
                fields
              end
    {status, reason, headers, lines_of.empty? ? nil : lines_of}
  end

  # A 1xx, 204 or 304: an answer with no content (RFC 9110 §15).
  def self.bodiless?(status : Int32) : Bool
    (status >= 100 && status < 200) || status == 204 || status == 304
  end

  private def self.header_in(headers : Hash(String, String), name : String) : String?
    headers.each do |key, value|
      return value if same_name?(key, name)
    end
    nil
  end

  # One request and its whole answer: the answer as `open` hands it, its
  # body read. Redirects are followed, *redirects* at most; *jar* keeps
  # the cookies the answers set and sends them back; *decompress* asks
  # for gzip, deflate and zstd and undoes them. A refused or reset
  # connection answers `SocketError`; a task cancelled answers
  # `Cancelled`; what the server does wrong answers `HTTPError`. `!`
  # passes any of them up, `.or_panic` refuses it.
  def self.request(method : String, url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, body : String | IyiIO = "", redirects : Int32 = 10, jar : CookieStore? = nil, decompress : Bool = true, timeout_ms : Int32 = 0, proxy : String? = nil) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    open(method, url, headers, body, redirects, jar, decompress, nil, timeout_ms, proxy) { |response| response.load }
  end

  # One request, and the block's answer to the response, its body still
  # arriving: `response.body_io.read` takes it a piece at a time, and
  # `response.body` all of it. The connection closes when the block
  # returns. A body of *body* is a string, sent with its length, or an
  # `IyiIO`, sent chunked as it is read - which a redirect that keeps the
  # method (307, 308) cannot send again, so that answer is handed to the
  # block as it is.
  #
  # The redirects followed are RFC 9110 §15.4's: 301 and 302 make a POST a
  # GET, 303 makes anything but a HEAD a GET, and 307 and 308 keep the
  # method and the body. `Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization` and
  # `Cookie` written by the caller are not sent to another scheme, host or
  # port - `https://` to `http://` at one host included, as curl does (Go
  # strips them for another host) - and a jar's `Secure` cookies go over
  # `https://` alone. The downgrade itself is followed, as Go's client and
  # `curl -L` follow it; any other header the caller wrote, and a 307 or
  # 308's body, go on in the clear. One more than *redirects* is an
  # `HTTPError`; with 0 the first answer is the block's.
  #
  # *timeout_ms* bounds the connect, and each read and write after it -
  # a pause, not the whole exchange, as Python's `timeout` is: a body that
  # keeps arriving is read for as long as it takes. One that runs out is a
  # `SocketError` whose `timed_out?` is true. 0, the default, waits as
  # long as the server does, as Go's and Python's clients do; a client
  # that talks to servers it does not control should give one.
  #
  # *proxy* is a proxy's URL - `http://user:password@host:port`, or
  # `host:port` alone - and every request goes through it, every redirect
  # too: `http://` as an absolute-form request to the proxy (RFC 9112
  # §3.2.2), which carries `Proxy-Authorization: Basic` when the URL has
  # a user; `https://` through a tunnel the proxy opens with `CONNECT
  # host:port` (RFC 9110 §9.3.6), the credential on the CONNECT alone and
  # TLS to the server inside it, its certificate checked as without a
  # proxy. A caller's own `Proxy-Authorization` is the proxy's too: sent
  # on the CONNECT when the URL has no user, and never inside the tunnel.
  # A CONNECT answered with anything but a 2xx - 407 when the proxy wants
  # credentials, 403, 502 - is an `HTTPError` naming the proxy and its
  # answer. `http://` through a proxy is HTTP/1.1 whatever
  # *prior_knowledge* says. Which proxy, if any, a URL's environment
  # names is `proxy_for`'s, and std/http_proxy's `HTTPProxy.from_env`.
  def self.open(method : String, url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, body : String | IyiIO = "", redirects : Int32 = 10, jar : CookieStore? = nil, decompress : Bool = true, client : HTTPClient? = nil, timeout_ms : Int32 = 0, proxy : String? = nil, &block : Response -> R) : R | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError forall R
    raise "HTTP: a timeout of #{timeout_ms} ms" if timeout_ms < 0
    route = ProxyRoute.of(proxy)
    return route if route.is_a?(HTTPError)
    current = url
    verb = method
    sent = headers
    payload = body
    hops = 0
    while true
      exchange = exchange(verb, current, sent, payload, jar, decompress, nil, client, timeout_ms, route)
      return exchange unless exchange.is_a?(Exchange)
      response = exchange.response
      location = response.header("Location")
      status = response.status
      redirect = location && (status == 301 || status == 302 || status == 303 || status == 307 || status == 308)
      keeps = status == 307 || status == 308
      if redirect && location && redirects > 0 && !(keeps && payload.is_a?(IyiIO))
        exchange.close
        return HTTPError.new("stopped after #{redirects} redirects, at #{current}") if hops == redirects
        target = redirected(current, location)
        return target if target.is_a?(HTTPError)
        unless keeps || (status != 303 && !same_name?(verb, "POST")) || same_name?(verb, "HEAD")
          verb = "GET"
          payload = ""
          sent = without(sent, ["Content-Type", "Content-Encoding"])
        end
        sent = without(sent, ["Authorization", "Proxy-Authorization", "Cookie"]) unless same_authority?(current, target)
        current = target
        hops = hops + 1
        next
      end
      return handed(exchange, block)
    end
  end

  # The 101 an upgrade to *protocol* is answered with, and the connection,
  # which is the caller's from then on: std/websocket's handshake. Any
  # other answer is an `HTTPError` naming it.
  #
  # *proxy* is `open`'s, and so is its credential, on the CONNECT alone;
  # but an upgrade goes through a tunnel for `http://` as for `https://`,
  # the handshake inside it in origin form. `Upgrade` belongs to the
  # connection it is sent on (RFC 9110 §7.8), and a proxy forwarding a
  # request removes `Connection` and every field it names (§7.6.1), so a
  # handshake handed to a forward proxy in absolute form reaches the
  # server, at best, as a plain GET: the gate's proxy, which drops
  # `Connection`, leaves std/websocket's server a request it answers 400.
  def self.upgrade(url : String, protocol : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, timeout_ms : Int32 = 0, proxy : String? = nil) : Upgraded | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    route = ProxyRoute.of(proxy)
    return route if route.is_a?(HTTPError)
    sent = headers.dup
    sent["Upgrade"] = protocol
    exchange = exchange("GET", url, sent, "", nil, false, "Upgrade", nil, timeout_ms, route)
    return exchange unless exchange.is_a?(Exchange)
    response = exchange.response
    if response.status != 101
      exchange.close
      return HTTPError.new("the server answered #{response.status} #{response.reason} to an upgrade to #{protocol}, not 101 Switching Protocols")
    end
    # The protocol after it waits as it likes.
    exchange.conn.unlimited
    Upgraded.new(response, exchange.conn.transport, exchange.conn.leftover)
  end

  private def self.handed(exchange : Exchange, block : Proc(Response, R)) : R forall R
    defer exchange.close
    block.call(exchange.response)
  end

  # *location* read against *current*: a redirect's target, or why it is
  # not one this client follows.
  private def self.redirected(current : String, location : String) : String | HTTPError
    base = URI.parse?(current)
    return HTTPError.new("a redirect from #{current.inspect}, which is not a URL") unless base
    target = URI.parse?(location)
    return HTTPError.new("a redirect to #{location.inspect}, which is not a URL") unless target
    resolved = base.resolve(target)
    scheme = resolved.scheme
    scheme = scheme.downcase if scheme
    return HTTPError.new("redirected to #{resolved}, which is not http:// or https://") unless scheme == "http" || scheme == "https"
    resolved.to_s
  end

  # The same scheme, host and port: a credential sent to one is not sent on
  # to another, nor from `https://` to `http://` at the same name.
  private def self.same_authority?(a : String, b : String) : Bool
    ua = URI.parse?(a)
    ub = URI.parse?(b)
    return false unless ua && ub
    sa = (ua.scheme || "").downcase
    sb = (ub.scheme || "").downcase
    return false unless sa == sb
    default = sa == "https" ? 443 : 80
    (ua.hostname || "").downcase == (ub.hostname || "").downcase && (ua.port || default) == (ub.port || default)
  end

  private def self.without(headers : Hash(String, String), names : Array(String)) : Hash(String, String)
    kept = {} of String => String
    headers.each do |name, value|
      dropped = false
      names.each { |n| dropped = true if same_name?(name, n) }
      kept[name] = value unless dropped
    end
    kept
  end

  # Connect - or take a connection *client* kept - write the request, read
  # the answer's head past any 1xx, and frame its body; the body itself is
  # still to come. *upgrade* is `Upgrade` for `HTTP.upgrade`; otherwise
  # the request says `close`, or `keep-alive` for a client. With *route*
  # the connection is the proxy's, and for `https://` a tunnel through it.
  private def self.exchange(method : String, url : String, headers : Hash(String, String), body : String | IyiIO, jar : CookieStore?, decompress : Bool, upgrade : String?, client : HTTPClient?, timeout_ms : Int32, route : ProxyRoute?) : Exchange | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    uri = URI.parse?(url)
    return HTTPError.new("not a URL: #{url.inspect}") unless uri
    scheme = uri.scheme
    # In any case (RFC 3986 §3.1): `HTTP://host/` was "only http://".
    scheme = scheme.downcase if scheme
    return HTTPError.new("only http:// and https://") unless scheme == "http" || scheme == "https"
    secure = scheme == "https"
    host = uri.hostname
    return HTTPError.new("missing host") unless host
    path = uri.path
    path = "/" if path.bytesize == 0
    if q = uri.query
      path = path + "?" + q
    end
    default_port = secure ? 443 : 80
    port = uri.port || default_port
    # An IPv6 literal in its brackets, as the URL wrote it (RFC 9110
    # §7.2): `hostname` is the address without them, and `Host: ::1:8080`
    # named no host a server can split from its port.
    named = host.includes?(":") ? "[#{host}]" : host
    host_line = port == default_port ? named : "#{named}:#{port}"
    sent = headers
    asked = decompress && header_in(headers, "Accept-Encoding").nil?
    cookies = nil.as(String?)
    if keeper = jar
      cookies = keeper.header_for(url)
    end
    if asked || cookies
      sent = headers.dup
      sent["Accept-Encoding"] = "gzip, deflate, zstd" if asked
      if cookies
        own = header_in(headers, "Cookie")
        sent = without(sent, ["Cookie"]) if own
        sent["Cookie"] = own ? own + "; " + cookies : cookies
      end
    end
    head = same_name?(method, "HEAD")
    key = (secure ? "https://" : "http://") + host.downcase + ":" + port.to_s
    dial_host = host
    dial_port = port
    target = path
    tunnel_credential = nil.as(String?)
    # `https://` goes through a tunnel, and so does an upgrade, which a
    # proxy does not forward (`upgrade`).
    tunneled = !route.nil? && (secure || !upgrade.nil?)
    if via = route
      dial_host = via.host
      dial_port = via.port
      if tunneled
        # A tunnel is one origin's, kept under the proxy and that origin.
        # `Proxy-Authorization` is the CONNECT's: the URL's, or else the
        # caller's, and never written inside the tunnel, where the origin
        # would read it.
        key = via.key + " " + key
        tunnel_credential = via.authorization || header_in(headers, "Proxy-Authorization")
        sent = without(sent, ["Proxy-Authorization"])
      else
        # A connection to the proxy carries a request for any origin, as
        # Go's and curl's do: kept under the proxy alone.
        key = via.key
        target = "http://" + host_line + path
        if credential = via.authorization
          sent = without(sent, ["Proxy-Authorization"])
          sent["Proxy-Authorization"] = credential
        end
      end
    end
    connection = upgrade || (client ? "keep-alive" : "close")
    # Sent again once, on a new connection, when a kept one turns out to
    # have been closed by the server before it answered.
    retry = body.is_a?(String) && (head || same_name?(method, "GET") || same_name?(method, "PUT") || same_name?(method, "DELETE") || same_name?(method, "OPTIONS") || same_name?(method, "TRACE"))
    refused_before = false
    while true
      conn = nil.as(Conn?)
      if keeping = client
        conn = keeping.take(key, secure ? tls_config : nil) unless upgrade
      end
      reused = !conn.nil?
      unless conn
        socket = IyiSocket.connect(dial_host, dial_port, timeout_ms > 0 ? timeout_ms : -1)!
        client.made if client
        if tunneled && (via = route)
          unopened = tunnel(socket, via, named + ":" + port.to_s, tunnel_credential, timeout_ms, secure)
          if unopened
            socket.close
            return unopened
          end
        end
        if secure
          # The handshake waits as long as a read does.
          if timeout_ms > 0
            socket.read_timeout_ms = timeout_ms
            socket.write_timeout_ms = timeout_ms
          end
          offered = tls_config
          if upgrade
            # An upgrade is HTTP/1.1's: ALPN offers that alone, as RFC
            # 8441's way over HTTP/2 (an extended CONNECT) is not here.
            offered = TlsConfig.new
            offered.roots = tls_config.roots
            offered.now = tls_config.now
            offered.identity = tls_config.identity
            offered.alpn = ["http/1.1"]
          end
          tls = TlsClient.over(socket, host, offered, timeout_ms > 0 ? timeout_ms : TlsClient::HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT_MS)
          return HTTPError.new(tls.message) if tls.is_a?(TlsError)
          conn = Conn.new(socket, stream: TlsStream.new(tls, host))
          conn.made_under = TlsMadeUnder.new(tls_config)
          speaks_h2 = upgrade.nil? && tls.alpn_protocol == "h2"
        else
          conn = Conn.new(socket)
          # Through a proxy the request is HTTP/1.1's absolute form.
          speaks_h2 = upgrade.nil? && !client.nil? && client.prior_knowledge && route.nil?
        end
        conn.gap = timeout_ms if timeout_ms > 0
        if speaks_h2
          h2 = H2Connection.new(conn, false)
          conn.h2 = h2
          unless h2.start
            conn.close
            return h2.failure || HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the connection could not be started")
          end
        end
      end
      if h2 = conn.h2
        answer = h2_exchange(conn, h2, method, secure ? "https" : "http", host_line, path, sent, body, url, head, asked, jar, client, key)
        return answer if answer.is_a?(Exchange)
        refused = h2.refused?
        if h2.usable?
          # One stream's trouble: the connection carries the next request.
          if keeping = client
            keeping.give_back(key, conn)
          else
            conn.close
          end
        else
          conn.close
          # A kept connection the server had begun to close: the request
          # is sent again on a new one, as over HTTP/1.1.
          next if reused && retry
        end
        # A refused stream was not processed (RFC 9113 §8.7): sent again
        # once, which an idempotent request with a string body may be.
        if refused && retry && !refused_before
          refused_before = true
          next
        end
        return answer
      end
      failed = send_request(conn.transport, method, target, host_line, sent, body, connection)
      if failed
        conn.close
        next if reused && retry
        return failed
      end
      answer = read_answer(conn, url, method, head, asked, jar, client, key)
      if answer.is_a?(Nil)
        conn.close
        next if reused && retry
        if fault = conn.failure
          return fault
        end
        return HTTPError.new("the connection ended before an answer")
      end
      conn.close unless answer.is_a?(Exchange)
      return answer
    end
  end

  # The tunnel to *authority* (`host:port`, an IPv6 host in brackets) on
  # *socket*, a connection to the proxy: `CONNECT` with the authority as
  # its target and its `Host` (RFC 9110 §9.3.6), *credential* as
  # `Proxy-Authorization`, and the proxy's answer read. Any 2xx opens the
  # tunnel and has no body (the RFC has a client ignore the framing such
  # an answer gives); anything else is refused with the proxy's status,
  # and so are bytes sent after a 2xx, before this end's TLS (*secure*)
  # or request has begun. Nil once the tunnel is open.
  private def self.tunnel(socket : IyiSocket, via : ProxyRoute, authority : String, credential : String?, timeout_ms : Int32, secure : Bool) : Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError | Nil
    ask = String::Builder.new
    ask << "CONNECT " << authority << " HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: " << authority << "\r\n"
    if credential
      raise "HTTP: header Proxy-Authorization contains a line break" if has_break?(credential)
      raise "HTTP: header Proxy-Authorization contains a control character" if control?(credential)
      ask << "Proxy-Authorization: " << credential << "\r\n"
    end
    ask << "\r\n"
    wrote = socket.write(ask.to_s)
    return wrote unless wrote.is_a?(Int32)
    conn = Conn.new(socket)
    conn.gap = timeout_ms if timeout_ms > 0
    got = conn.read_head(Server::MAX_HEAD, "the proxy's response")
    case got
    in Nil
      if fault = conn.failure
        return fault
      end
      return HTTPError.new("the proxy at #{via.label} closed the connection before it answered CONNECT #{authority}", 502)
    in HTTPError
      return got
    in String
      parsed = parse_response_head(got)
      return parsed if parsed.is_a?(HTTPError)
      status, reason, _, _ = parsed
      unless status >= 200 && status < 300
        return HTTPError.new("the proxy at #{via.label} answered #{status} #{reason} to CONNECT #{authority}", 502)
      end
      if conn.available > 0
        return HTTPError.new("the proxy at #{via.label} sent #{conn.available} bytes after its #{status} to CONNECT #{authority}, before #{secure ? "TLS" : "the request"} began", 502)
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  # The proxy a request for *url* goes through, given the values of
  # `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` - nil when it goes
  # direct. Go's `golang.org/x/net/http/httpproxy` is the rule, line for
  # line, as its documentation and tests state it:
  #
  # * `https://` takes *https_proxy*, `http://` *http_proxy*, any other
  #   scheme none. A proxy written without a scheme is `http://`'s, and is
  #   answered with it, ready for `open`'s *proxy*.
  # * `localhost` (in lower case: Go's comparison) and a loopback address
  #   (127.0.0.0/8, ::1, the first as IPv4-mapped too) never go through a
  #   proxy.
  # * *no_proxy* is a comma-separated list, each entry trimmed and read
  #   without regard to case: `*` alone sends everything direct; an IP
  #   address or a CIDR range (`10.0.0.0/8`, `2001:db8::/32`) matches the
  #   addresses in it, IPv4 and its IPv4-mapped form alike, and a CIDR
  #   range of one family never the other's; a domain matches itself and
  #   every name under it (`foo.com`: `foo.com`, `bar.foo.com`, not
  #   `barfoo.com`); with a leading dot, or `*.`, only the names under it
  #   (`.y.com`: `x.y.com`, not `y.com`). An address or domain may carry
  #   a port (`1.2.3.4:80`, `[::1]:443`, `foo.com:8080`), and matches
  #   then on that port alone, the URL's own or its scheme's default.
  #   An entry that is none of these (`:80`) is passed over.
  # * A host is compared as IDNA's ASCII form, as Go compares it.
  #
  # Pure: the values are the caller's, so std/http reaches no environment;
  # std/http_proxy reads them from it.
  def self.proxy_for(url : String, http_proxy : String?, https_proxy : String?, no_proxy : String? = nil) : String?
    uri = URI.parse?(url)
    return nil unless uri
    scheme = (uri.scheme || "").downcase
    chosen = if scheme == "https"
               https_proxy
             elsif scheme == "http"
               http_proxy
             else
               nil
             end
    return nil unless chosen && chosen.bytesize > 0
    host = uri.hostname || ""
    port = uri.port || (scheme == "https" ? 443 : 80)
    return nil unless proxied?(host, port.to_s, no_proxy || "")
    chosen.includes?("://") ? chosen : "http://" + chosen
  end

  # Whether *host* on *port* goes through a proxy under *no_proxy*: Go's
  # `useProxy`, `proxy_for`'s rules.
  private def self.proxied?(host : String, port : String, no_proxy : String) : Bool
    ascii = ascii_name(host)
    # `localhost` exactly as written, before the name is lowered: Go's
    # rule, which sends `LOCALHOST` through the proxy (its NO_PROXY
    # entries and names are compared lowered).
    return false if ascii == "localhost"
    name = ascii.downcase
    ip = address_of(name)
    return false if ip && loopback?(ip)
    no_proxy.split(',').each do |entry|
      written = trim(entry).downcase
      next if written.bytesize == 0
      return false if written == "*"
      if slash = written.index('/')
        net = address_of(written[0, slash])
        bits = prefix_bits(written[slash + 1, written.bytesize - slash - 1])
        # A dotted address's length counts IPv4's 32 bits, the last of
        # the 128 its mapped form holds. A length past the family's is no
        # range, and the entry is read on as Go reads it on.
        dotted = !written[0, slash].includes?(":")
        if net && bits && bits <= (dotted ? 32 : 128)
          return false if ip && in_range?(ip, net, dotted ? bits + 96 : bits)
          next
        end
      end
      phost = written
      pport = ""
      if split = split_host_port(written)
        phost, pport = split
        next if phost.bytesize == 0
      end
      if pip = address_of(phost)
        return false if ip && ip == pip && (pport.bytesize == 0 || pport == port)
        next
      end
      next if phost.bytesize == 0
      phost = phost[1, phost.bytesize - 1] if phost.starts_with?("*.")
      itself = !phost.starts_with?(".")
      phost = phost[1, phost.bytesize - 1] unless itself
      suffix = "." + ascii_name(phost)
      if name.ends_with?(suffix) || (itself && name == suffix[1, suffix.bytesize - 1])
        return false if pport.bytesize == 0 || pport == port
      end
    end
    true
  end

  # *host* as IDNA's ASCII form, as Go's `idna.Lookup` makes it: a name
  # in ASCII as it is, capitals and all (Go lowers it after, and compares
  # `localhost` before); one beyond ASCII lowered first - UTS #46 maps
  # capitals, which std/idna's IDNA2008 refuses - and carried by
  # `IDNA.to_ascii`; as it is when it has no such form.
  private def self.ascii_name(host : String) : String
    raw = host.to_unsafe
    at = 0
    while at < host.bytesize && raw[at] < 128_u8
      at = at + 1
    end
    return host if at == host.bytesize || !Unicode.valid?(host)
    ascii = URI.ascii_host(Unicode.downcase(host))
    ascii.is_a?(String) ? ascii : host
  end

  # An IP address as its sixteen bytes, IPv4 as its IPv4-mapped form
  # (`::ffff:1.2.3.4`), as Go's `net.IP` holds one; nil for a name.
  private def self.address_of(text : String) : {UInt64, UInt64}?
    if text.includes?(":")
      six = IyiSocket.read_ipv6(text)
      return six.is_a?(String) ? nil : {six.high, six.low}
    end
    four = IyiSocket.read_ipv4(text)
    return nil if four.is_a?(String)
    {0_u64, 0xffff00000000_u64 | four.b0.to_u64.unsafe_shl(24_u64) | four.b1.to_u64.unsafe_shl(16_u64) | four.b2.to_u64.unsafe_shl(8_u64) | four.b3.to_u64}
  end

  private def self.mapped?(ip : {UInt64, UInt64}) : Bool
    ip[0] == 0_u64 && ip[1].unsafe_shr(32_u64) == 0xffff_u64
  end

  private def self.loopback?(ip : {UInt64, UInt64}) : Bool
    return ip[1].unsafe_shr(24_u64) & 0xff_u64 == 127_u64 if mapped?(ip)
    ip[0] == 0_u64 && ip[1] == 1_u64
  end

  # Whether *ip* is in the range of *net*'s first *bits* bits (of 128).
  # The range is IPv4's when its address, cut to those bits, is still
  # IPv4-mapped, and then holds IPv4 addresses alone; an IPv6 range holds
  # IPv6 addresses alone - Go's `ParseCIDR` and `IPNet.Contains`, which
  # compare a four-byte form with a sixteen-byte one as different.
  private def self.in_range?(ip : {UInt64, UInt64}, net : {UInt64, UInt64}, bits : Int32) : Bool
    high_mask = bits >= 64 ? ~0_u64 : (bits == 0 ? 0_u64 : (~0_u64).unsafe_shl((64 - bits).to_u64))
    low_mask = bits <= 64 ? 0_u64 : (bits == 128 ? ~0_u64 : (~0_u64).unsafe_shl((128 - bits).to_u64))
    cut = {net[0] & high_mask, net[1] & low_mask}
    return false unless mapped?(ip) == mapped?(cut)
    (ip[0] & high_mask) == cut[0] && (ip[1] & low_mask) == cut[1]
  end

  # A CIDR prefix length: decimal digits, nil otherwise.
  private def self.prefix_bits(text : String) : Int32?
    return nil if text.bytesize == 0 || text.bytesize > 3
    return nil unless digits?(text)
    text.to_i
  end

  # Go's `net.SplitHostPort`: `host:port` or `[host]:port`, the port
  # possibly empty; nil where it has no port, or too many colons.
  private def self.split_host_port(text : String) : {String, String}?
    raw = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    colon = -1
    i = 0
    while i < n
      colon = i if raw[i] == 58_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    return nil if colon < 0
    if n > 0 && raw[0] == 91_u8
      close = text.index(']')
      return nil unless close && close + 1 == colon
      host = text[1, close - 1]
      return nil if host.includes?("[") || text[close + 1, n - close - 1].includes?("]")
      return {host, text[colon + 1, n - colon - 1]}
    end
    host = text[0, colon]
    return nil if host.includes?(":") || host.includes?("[") || host.includes?("]")
    port = text[colon + 1, n - colon - 1]
    return nil if port.includes?("[") || port.includes?("]")
    {host, port}
  end

  # A request over HTTP/2: a stream of its own on *h2*, the answer's final
  # head read, and its body framed by the stream's DATA. The fields come
  # back as an HTTP/1.1 answer's do, a name on several fields one field
  # with its values joined by ", " and kept apart in `header_values`; an
  # HTTP/2 answer has no reason phrase, so the status's own is given.
  private def self.h2_exchange(conn : Conn, h2 : H2Connection, method : String, scheme : String, authority : String, path : String, sent : Hash(String, String), body : String | IyiIO, url : String, head : Bool, asked : Bool, jar : CookieStore?, client : HTTPClient?, key : String) : Exchange | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    id = h2.send_request(method, scheme, authority, path, sent, body)
    return id unless id.is_a?(Int32)
    fields = h2.answer_head(id)
    unless fields.is_a?(Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField))
      h2.forget(id)
      return fields
    end
    status = 0
    headers = {} of String => String
    apart = {} of String => Array(String)
    fields.each do |f|
      if f.name == ":status"
        status = f.value.to_i
        next
      end
      if had = headers[f.name]?
        if values = apart[f.name]?
          values << f.value
        else
          apart[f.name] = [had, f.value]
        end
      else
        headers[f.name] = f.value
      end
    end
    apart.each { |name, values| headers[name] = values.join(", ") }
    s = h2.stream(id)
    unlimited = 9223372036854775807_i64
    empty = head || bodiless?(status) || (!s.nil? && s.ended && s.pieces.empty?)
    reader = empty ? BodyReader.new(nil, BodyReader::EMPTY, 0_i64, unlimited) : BodyReader.frames(h2, id, unlimited)
    decoded = false
    if asked && !reader.finished?
      decoded = undo_coding(reader, headers)
      if decoded.is_a?(HTTPError)
        h2.forget(id)
        return decoded
      end
    end
    response = Response.new(status, reason_for(status), headers, apart.empty? ? nil : apart, reader, url, decoded)
    if keeper = jar
      keeper.update(url, response)
    end
    exchange = Exchange.new(conn, response, client, key, true)
    exchange.h2_id = id
    exchange
  end

  # The request on *socket*: its head, and a string body with it or an
  # `IyiIO` body chunked after it. Nil once written, or why not.
  private def self.send_request(socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream, method : String, path : String, host_line : String, sent : Hash(String, String), body : String | IyiIO, connection : String) : Cancelled | SocketError | Nil
    case body
    in String
      length = body.bytesize > 0 || same_name?(method, "POST") || same_name?(method, "PUT") || same_name?(method, "PATCH") ? body.bytesize.to_i64 : nil
      text = request_head(method, path, host_line, sent, length, false, connection)
      wrote = socket.write(body.bytesize > 0 && body.bytesize < 65536 ? text + body : text)
      return wrote unless wrote.is_a?(Int32)
      if body.bytesize >= 65536
        wrote = socket.write(body)
        return wrote unless wrote.is_a?(Int32)
      end
    in IyiIO
      wrote = socket.write(request_head(method, path, host_line, sent, nil, true, connection))
      return wrote unless wrote.is_a?(Int32)
      writer = BodyWriter.new(socket, true)
      while true
        piece = body.read_bytes(65536)
        break if piece.bytesize == 0
        failed = writer.write(piece)
        return failed unless failed.nil?
      end
      finished = writer.finish
      return finished unless finished.nil?
    end
    nil
  end

  # The answer on *conn*: its head read past any 1xx and its body framed.
  # Nil when the connection ends before a byte of one, which on a kept
  # connection is the server having closed it while it waited.
  private def self.read_answer(conn : Conn, url : String, method : String, head : Bool, asked : Bool, jar : CookieStore?, client : HTTPClient?, key : String) : Exchange | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError | Nil
    while true
      # As far as the server's limit on a request's head, and no further: a
      # head that did not end was read for as long as it came, 256 MB of one
      # field cost the client 824 MB, and one that never ended took it all.
      got = conn.read_head(Server::MAX_HEAD, "response")
      case got
      in Nil
        return HTTPError.new("no header terminator") if conn.available > 0
        if fault = conn.failure
          return fault unless fault.is_a?(SocketError)
        end
        return nil
      in HTTPError
        return got
      in String
        parsed = parse_response_head(got)
        return parsed if parsed.is_a?(HTTPError)
        status, reason, fields, apart = parsed
        next if status >= 100 && status < 200 && status != 101
        reader = body_framing(conn, status, fields, head)
        return reader if reader.is_a?(HTTPError)
        decoded = false
        if asked && !reader.finished?
          decoded = undo_coding(reader, fields)
          return decoded if decoded.is_a?(HTTPError)
        end
        response = Response.new(status, reason, fields, apart, reader, url, decoded)
        if keeper = jar
          keeper.update(url, response)
        end
        keep = false
        if client
          said = header_in(fields, "Connection")
          keep = got.starts_with?("HTTP/1.1 ") && !reader.to_close? && status != 101 && (said.nil? || !has_token?(said, "close"))
        end
        return Exchange.new(conn, response, client, key, keep)
      end
    end
  end

  # How an answer's body is framed (RFC 9112 §6.3): none for a HEAD, a
  # 1xx, a 204 or a 304; chunks; a length; or the connection's close.
  private def self.body_framing(conn : Conn, status : Int32, fields : Hash(String, String), head : Bool) : BodyReader | HTTPError
    unlimited = 9223372036854775807_i64
    return BodyReader.new(conn, BodyReader::EMPTY, 0_i64, unlimited) if head || bodiless?(status) || status == 101
    if te = header_in(fields, "Transfer-Encoding")
      if refused = coding_refused(te)
        return HTTPError.new(refused)
      end
      return BodyReader.new(conn, BodyReader::CHUNKED, 0_i64, unlimited)
    end
    if cl = header_in(fields, "Content-Length")
      n = content_length(cl)
      return HTTPError.new("Content-Length is not a number: #{cl.inspect}") unless n
      return BodyReader.new(conn, BodyReader::LENGTH, n, unlimited)
    end
    BodyReader.new(conn, BodyReader::CLOSE, 0_i64, unlimited)
  end

  # The answer's `Content-Encoding` undone as the body is read, when it is
  # one coding this client asked for: gzip (and its old name x-gzip),
  # deflate - zlib's envelope, as RFC 9110 §8.4.1.2 says, or the bare
  # stream some servers send under the name, told apart by zlib's header
  # check - or zstd, with a window of 8 MiB at most: RFC 9659 §3 holds
  # HTTP's zstd to that, and a frame that asks for more is refused rather
  # than given the memory. The fields that described the coded body go.
  # Another coding is left as it came, for the caller, with the headers
  # that say so.
  private def self.undo_coding(reader : BodyReader, fields : Hash(String, String)) : Bool | HTTPError
    coding = header_in(fields, "Content-Encoding")
    return false unless coding
    name = trim(coding).downcase
    if name == "zstd"
      reader.decode_zstd(Zstd::WINDOW_LIMIT)
    else
      envelope = if name == "gzip" || name == "x-gzip"
                   Envelope::Gzip
                 elsif name == "deflate"
                   Envelope::Zlib
                 else
                   return false
                 end
      reader.decode(envelope)
    end
    fields.reject_in_place { |key, _| same_name?(key, "Content-Encoding") || same_name?(key, "Content-Length") }
    true
  end

  def self.get(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("GET", url, headers)
  end

  def self.head(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("HEAD", url, headers)
  end

  def self.post(url : String, body : String | IyiIO = "", headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("POST", url, headers, body)
  end

  def self.put(url : String, body : String | IyiIO = "", headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("PUT", url, headers, body)
  end

  def self.delete(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String) : Response | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    request("DELETE", url, headers)
  end

  # *text* in gzip, whole: an answer's body under a server's `compress`.
  def self.gzip(text : String) : String
    Gzip.compress(text)
  end

  # A writer whose body goes out in gzip as it is written.
  def self.gzip_writer(socket : IyiSocket | TlsStream | H2Out, chunked : Bool) : BodyWriter
    BodyWriter.new(socket, chunked, Deflater.new(Envelope::Gzip))
  end

  # Whether a list of codings a client accepts (`Accept-Encoding`, RFC
  # 9110 §12.5.3) takes *coding*: named, or `*`, with a `q` above 0.
  def self.accepts?(list : String, coding : String) : Bool
    coding_weight(list, coding) > 0
  end

  # How much a list of codings a client accepts wants *coding*, as its
  # `q` in thousandths: the weight it is named with, else `*`'s, else 0 -
  # and 0 refuses it.
  def self.coding_weight(list : String, coding : String) : Int32
    starred = 0
    list.split(',').each do |part|
      item = trim(part)
      next if item.bytesize == 0
      name = item
      weight = 1000
      if semi = item.index(';')
        name = trim(item[0, semi])
        params = item[semi + 1, item.bytesize - semi - 1]
        params.split(';').each do |param|
          kv = trim(param)
          if kv.bytesize > 2 && (kv.starts_with?("q=") || kv.starts_with?("Q="))
            weight = weight_of(kv[2, kv.bytesize - 2])
          end
        end
      end
      return weight if same_name?(name, coding)
      starred = weight if name == "*"
    end
    starred
  end

  # A `q` in thousandths (RFC 9110 §12.4.2: 0 to 1, three decimals at
  # most), `0.5` 500. A `q` that is not one of these is not a refusal,
  # and is read as 1, unless it is all zeros.
  private def self.weight_of(text : String) : Int32
    return 0 if zero_weight?(text)
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    return 1000 unless p[0] == 48_u8 && n > 2 && n <= 5 && p[1] == 46_u8
    value = 0
    scale = 100
    i = 2
    while i < n
      return 1000 unless p[i] >= 48_u8 && p[i] <= 57_u8
      value = value + (p[i] - 48_u8).to_i32 * scale
      scale = scale // 10
      i = i + 1
    end
    value
  end

  # A `q` of 0, `0.`, `0.000`: the one weight that refuses (RFC 9110
  # §12.4.2); anything else is some preference, and a `q` that is not a
  # number is not a refusal either.
  private def self.zero_weight?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    return false unless text.bytesize > 0 && p[0] == 48_u8
    i = 1
    while i < text.bytesize
      return false unless p[i] == 48_u8 || p[i] == 46_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  # The pairs of a request's `Cookie` header, `a=1; b=2` (RFC 6265 §4.2),
  # a value's quotes taken off; the first of a name is kept, as a server
  # reads the most specific cookie first.
  def self.cookie_pairs(value : String) : Hash(String, String)
    found = {} of String => String
    value.split(';').each do |part|
      eq = part.index('=')
      next unless eq
      name = trim(part[0, eq])
      next if name.bytesize == 0
      text = trim(part[eq + 1, part.bytesize - eq - 1])
      text = text[1, text.bytesize - 2] if text.bytesize >= 2 && text.starts_with?("\"") && text.ends_with?("\"")
      found[name] = text unless found.has_key?(name)
    end
    found
  end

  # *n* in lowercase hexadecimal: a chunk's size line.
  def self.hex_text(n : Int64) : String
    return "0" if n == 0
    digits = "0123456789abcdef".to_unsafe
    count = 0
    probe = n
    while probe > 0
      count = count + 1
      probe = probe >> 4
    end
    String.new(count) do |buf|
      i = count - 1
      value = n
      while i >= 0
        buf[i] = digits[(value & 15_i64).to_i32]
        value = value >> 4
        i = i - 1
      end
    end
  end

  # Why an answer's `Transfer-Encoding` is not one this client decodes,
  # nil when it is `chunked` alone: it undoes no other coding, and chunked
  # twice is a body framed twice. An empty list element is no coding (RFC
  # 9110 §5.6.1).
  private def self.coding_refused(te : String) : String?
    chunked = 0
    te.split(',').each do |part|
      coding = trim(part)
      next if coding.bytesize == 0
      return "transfer coding #{coding.inspect} is not one this client decodes" unless same_name?(coding, "chunked")
      chunked = chunked + 1
    end
    return "Transfer-Encoding #{te.inspect} names no coding this client decodes" if chunked == 0
    return "Transfer-Encoding #{te.inspect} is chunked twice" if chunked > 1
    nil
  end

  # The field lines after the first, or why they are not fields. A name
  # repeated, in any case, is one field, its values joined in order with
  # ", " (RFC 9110 §5.3): into a Hash by name, a second line spelled the
  # same replaced the first and one spelled otherwise was an entry
  # `header` never reached, so two different `Content-Length`s were read
  # as whichever came first or last. A value with a control character in
  # it - a CR or LF that ends no line, a NUL - is refused (§5.5) rather than
  # handed to whoever reads it or echoes it into a header.
  #
  # *unfold*, for an answer: a line that begins with a blank continues the
  # field above it (obs-fold, RFC 9112 §5.2), and a user agent joins it on
  # with one space; read as a field of its own, `HTTP.get` panicked with
  # "header without a colon: \" b\"". A server may refuse one, and this
  # one does.
  #
  # *apart*, when given, is handed each repeated field's values by the
  # name in the answer, a line each, before they are joined.
  def self.parse_fields(lines : Array(String), unfold : Bool = false, apart : Hash(String, Array(String))? = nil) : Hash(String, String) | String
    lines = unfolded(lines) if unfold
    headers = {} of String => String
    # The name as it was first spelled, by its lowercase, and the values
    # after the first, joined once at the end: joining line by line copied
    # the value so far per line.
    spelled = {} of String => String
    more = apart || {} of String => Array(String)
    i = 1
    while i < lines.size
      line = lines[i]
      i = i + 1
      next if line.bytesize == 0
      colon = line.index(':')
      return "header without a colon: #{line.inspect}" unless colon
      name = line[0, colon]
      return "not a header name: #{name.inspect}" unless token?(name)
      value = trim(line[colon + 1, line.bytesize - colon - 1])
      return "header #{name} contains a control character" if control?(value)
      key = name.downcase
      if first = spelled[key]?
        if values = more[first]?
          values << value
        else
          more[first] = [headers[first], value]
        end
      else
        spelled[key] = name
        headers[name] = value
      end
    end
    more.each do |name, values|
      headers[name] = values.join(", ")
    end
    headers
  end

  # *lines* with each field's folded lines joined onto it, one space for
  # each fold. A blank-led line right under the status line continues no
  # field, and is left for `parse_fields` to refuse.
  private def self.unfolded(lines : Array(String)) : Array(String)
    out = [] of String
    i = 0
    while i < lines.size
      line = lines[i]
      i = i + 1
      if i > 1 && i < lines.size && folded?(lines[i])
        joined = String::Builder.new
        joined << trim(line)
        while i < lines.size && folded?(lines[i])
          joined << ' ' << trim(lines[i])
          i = i + 1
        end
        line = joined.to_s
      end
      out << line
    end
    out
  end

  private def self.folded?(line : String) : Bool
    line.bytesize > 0 && (line.to_unsafe[0] == 32_u8 || line.to_unsafe[0] == 9_u8)
  end

  # A `Content-Length` as a number, or nil when it is not one. Repeated
  # lines arrive joined, and are one length only when every one says the
  # same (RFC 9112 §6.3). A length past 2147483647 is still a length, too
  # large for either side, and is read as one past it: `to_i?` answered
  # nil for it, and 3000000000 was refused as "not a number".
  def self.content_length(value : String) : Int64?
    parts = value.split(',')
    return nil if parts.size == 0
    first = trim(parts[0])
    parts.each do |part|
      return nil unless trim(part) == first
    end
    # Digits and nothing else (RFC 9110 §8.6): `to_i?` took a sign, and
    # `+2` was a length.
    return nil unless first.bytesize > 0 && digits?(first)
    p = first.to_unsafe
    n = 0_i64
    i = 0
    while i < first.bytesize
      n = n * 10_i64 + (p[i] - 48_u8).to_i64
      return 2147483648_i64 if n > 2147483647_i64
      i = i + 1
    end
    n
  end

  # A chunked body (RFC 9112 §7.1) as one string: each chunk's size line
  # is hexadecimal, extensions after `;` are ignored, and a zero-length
  # chunk ends it. What follows the last chunk is trailers, which nothing
  # here reads.
  def self.decode_chunked(text : String) : String
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    # One builder for the body: adding each chunk to the body so far copied
    # it once per chunk.
    out = String::Builder.new
    i = 0
    while true
      line_end = find_crlf(text, i)
      raise "HTTP: chunked body ends inside a chunk size" unless line_end
      size_text = text[i, line_end - i]
      if semi = size_text.index(';')
        size_text = size_text[0, semi]
      end
      size = hex(trim(size_text))
      raise "HTTP: not a chunk size: #{size_text.inspect}" unless size
      i = line_end + 2
      break if size == 0
      # `size` may be 2^31 - 1: `i + size` passed Int32's end and panicked.
      raise "HTTP: chunked body ends inside a chunk" if size > n - i
      out.write(p + i, size)
      i = i + size
      raise "HTTP: a chunk is not followed by CRLF" unless i + 1 < n && p[i] == 13_u8 && p[i + 1] == 10_u8
      i = i + 2
    end
    out.to_s
  end

  # Where *text* goes on at *from* past the empty lines there: what a
  # server skips before a request line (RFC 9112 §2.2).
  def self.past_empty_lines(text : String, from : Int32) : Int32
    p = text.to_unsafe
    at = from
    while at + 1 < text.bytesize && p[at] == 13_u8 && p[at + 1] == 10_u8
      at = at + 2
    end
    at
  end

  def self.find_headers_end(text : String, from : Int32 = 0) : Int32?
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    i = from
    while i + 3 < n
      if p[i] == 13_u8 && p[i + 1] == 10_u8 && p[i + 2] == 13_u8 && p[i + 3] == 10_u8
        return i
      end
      i = i + 1
    end
    nil
  end

  def self.split_crlf(text : String) : Array(String)
    lines = [] of String
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    start = 0
    i = 0
    while i < n
      if p[i] == 13_u8 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] == 10_u8
        lines << text[start, i - start]
        i = i + 2
        start = i
      else
        i = i + 1
      end
    end
    lines << text[start, n - start] if start < n
    lines
  end

  # Two field names for the same field: ASCII case is the only difference
  # a name may carry.
  def self.same_name?(a : String, b : String) : Bool
    return false unless a.bytesize == b.bytesize
    pa = a.to_unsafe
    pb = b.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < a.bytesize
      ca = pa[i]
      cb = pb[i]
      ca = ca + 32_u8 if ca >= 65_u8 && ca <= 90_u8
      cb = cb + 32_u8 if cb >= 65_u8 && cb <= 90_u8
      return false unless ca == cb
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  # Whether a list of tokens (RFC 9110 §5.6.1), its repeated lines joined
  # with ", ", holds *token*, in any case.
  def self.has_token?(list : String, token : String) : Bool
    list.split(',').each do |part|
      return true if same_name?(trim(part), token)
    end
    false
  end

  # A token (RFC 9110 §5.6.2): what a method or a field name is made of.
  def self.token?(text : String) : Bool
    return false if text.bytesize == 0
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      c = p[i]
      letter = (c >= 65_u8 && c <= 90_u8) || (c >= 97_u8 && c <= 122_u8)
      digit = c >= 48_u8 && c <= 57_u8
      mark = c == 33_u8 || (c >= 35_u8 && c <= 39_u8) || c == 42_u8 || c == 43_u8 ||
             c == 45_u8 || c == 46_u8 || c == 94_u8 || c == 95_u8 || c == 96_u8 ||
             c == 124_u8 || c == 126_u8
      return false unless letter || digit || mark
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  def self.has_break?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      return true if p[i] == 13_u8 || p[i] == 10_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    false
  end

  # A byte a request line may not carry: a blank, which ends its part, or
  # a control character.
  private def self.has_space_or_control?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      return true if p[i] <= 32_u8 || p[i] == 127_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    false
  end

  # *target* with each byte past 127 written `%XX`, as a browser and Go's
  # client send it; the target itself when it has none.
  private def self.ascii_target(target : String) : String
    p = target.to_unsafe
    n = target.bytesize
    high = 0
    i = 0
    while i < n
      high = high + 1 if p[i] > 127_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    return target if high == 0
    digits = "0123456789ABCDEF".to_unsafe
    String.new(n + high * 2) do |buf|
      at = 0
      i = 0
      while i < n
        b = p[i]
        if b > 127_u8
          buf[at] = 37_u8
          buf[at + 1] = digits[b.to_i32 // 16]
          buf[at + 2] = digits[b.to_i32 % 16]
          at = at + 3
        else
          buf[at] = b
          at = at + 1
        end
        i = i + 1
      end
    end
  end

  # A control character other than a tab, or DEL: what a field value may
  # not hold (RFC 9110 §5.5), nor a request target or a reason phrase.
  def self.control?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      return true if (p[i] < 32_u8 && p[i] != 9_u8) || p[i] == 127_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    false
  end

  # For a request target in absolute form (RFC 9112 §3.2.2), where its
  # authority starts: after `http://` or `https://`, in any case.
  def self.absolute_form(target : String) : Int32?
    return 7 if target.bytesize >= 7 && same_name?(target[0, 7], "http://")
    return 8 if target.bytesize >= 8 && same_name?(target[0, 8], "https://")
    nil
  end

  # A Host value - the `a` of `Host: a` - or an absolute-form target's
  # authority, made of what a host and its port are spelled with (RFC 3986
  # §3.2.2, §3.2.3): a name, an IPv4 address or an IPv6 one in brackets,
  # and digits after a colon. Empty is one too, for a target with no
  # authority (RFC 9112 §3.2). A blank, a `/`, an `@` - the bytes another
  # host or a path begins with - are not.
  def self.host?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      c = p[i]
      letter = (c >= 65_u8 && c <= 90_u8) || (c >= 97_u8 && c <= 122_u8)
      digit = c >= 48_u8 && c <= 57_u8
      # ! $ % & ' ( ) * + , - . : ; = [ ] _ ~
      mark = c == 33_u8 || (c >= 36_u8 && c <= 46_u8) || c == 58_u8 || c == 59_u8 ||
             c == 61_u8 || c == 91_u8 || c == 93_u8 || c == 95_u8 || c == 126_u8
      return false unless letter || digit || mark
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  private def self.find_crlf(text : String, from : Int32) : Int32?
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    i = from
    while i + 1 < n
      return i if p[i] == 13_u8 && p[i + 1] == 10_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    nil
  end

  # The value of a chunk size's hex digits, nil for anything else or for
  # one past what an Int32 holds.
  def self.hex(text : String) : Int32?
    return nil if text.bytesize == 0
    p = text.to_unsafe
    value = 0
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      c = p[i]
      digit = if c >= 48_u8 && c <= 57_u8
                (c - 48_u8).to_i32
              elsif c >= 97_u8 && c <= 102_u8
                (c - 87_u8).to_i32
              elsif c >= 65_u8 && c <= 70_u8
                (c - 55_u8).to_i32
              else
                return nil
              end
      return nil if value > 0x7FFFFFF
      value = value * 16 + digit
      i = i + 1
    end
    value
  end

  # A status line read: the status and its reason, or why it is not one.
  def self.status_line(line : String) : {Int32, String} | HTTPError
    # HTTP/1.1 200 OK: the version is `HTTP/` DIGIT "." DIGIT and the
    # status three digits from 100 (RFC 9112 §2.3, §4). Read by `to_i?`, a
    # sign was a digit, so `+20` and `-99` were statuses, and so were 000
    # to 099; `HTTP/x` was a version, and a reason could hold a bare CR.
    return HTTPError.new("not a status line: #{line}") unless line.starts_with?("HTTP/")
    sp1 = line.index(' ')
    return HTTPError.new("not a status line: #{line}") unless sp1 && sp1 == 8 && digits?(line[5, 1]) && line.to_unsafe[6] == 46_u8 && digits?(line[7, 1])
    rest = line[sp1 + 1, line.bytesize - sp1 - 1]
    sp2 = rest.index(' ')
    code_text = sp2 ? rest[0, sp2] : rest
    code = code_text.to_i?
    return HTTPError.new("not a status line: #{line}") unless code && code_text.bytesize == 3 && digits?(code_text) && code >= 100
    reason = sp2 ? rest[sp2 + 1, rest.bytesize - sp2 - 1] : ""
    return HTTPError.new("not a status line: #{line}") if control?(reason)
    {code, reason}
  end

  private def self.digits?(text : String) : Bool
    p = text.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      return false unless p[i] >= 48_u8 && p[i] <= 57_u8
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  # The reason phrase a status carries (RFC 9110 §15), for a response the
  # server writes; a status this table does not know gets its class's.
  def self.reason_for(status : Int32) : String
    case status
    when 100 then "Continue"
    when 101 then "Switching Protocols"
    when 200 then "OK"
    when 201 then "Created"
    when 202 then "Accepted"
    when 204 then "No Content"
    when 206 then "Partial Content"
    when 301 then "Moved Permanently"
    when 302 then "Found"
    when 303 then "See Other"
    when 304 then "Not Modified"
    when 307 then "Temporary Redirect"
    when 308 then "Permanent Redirect"
    when 400 then "Bad Request"
    when 401 then "Unauthorized"
    when 403 then "Forbidden"
    when 404 then "Not Found"
    when 405 then "Method Not Allowed"
    when 408 then "Request Timeout"
    when 409 then "Conflict"
    when 410 then "Gone"
    when 411 then "Length Required"
    when 412 then "Precondition Failed"
    when 413 then "Content Too Large"
    when 415 then "Unsupported Media Type"
    when 416 then "Range Not Satisfiable"
    when 426 then "Upgrade Required"
    when 429 then "Too Many Requests"
    when 431 then "Request Header Fields Too Large"
    when 500 then "Internal Server Error"
    when 501 then "Not Implemented"
    when 502 then "Bad Gateway"
    when 503 then "Service Unavailable"
    when 504 then "Gateway Timeout"
    else
      if status < 200
        "Informational"
      elsif status < 300
        "Success"
      elsif status < 400
        "Redirection"
      elsif status < 500
        "Client Error"
      else
        "Server Error"
      end
    end
  end

  # Where a chunked body that starts at *from* ends in `text` — the byte
  # after the last chunk's terminating CRLF (trailers included) — nil when
  # it has not all arrived, or why it is not a chunked body. What
  # `decode_chunked` will then read whole.
  def self.chunked_end(text : String, from : Int32 = 0) : Int32 | String | Nil
    p = text.to_unsafe
    n = text.bytesize
    i = from
    while true
      line_end = find_crlf(text, i)
      return nil unless line_end
      size_text = text[i, line_end - i]
      if semi = size_text.index(';')
        size_text = size_text[0, semi]
      end
      size = hex(trim(size_text))
      return "not a chunk size: #{size_text.inspect}" unless size
      i = line_end + 2
      if size == 0
        # Trailers, then a blank line.
        while true
          t_end = find_crlf(text, i)
          return nil unless t_end
          return t_end + 2 if t_end == i
          i = t_end + 2
        end
      end
      # `size` may be 2^31 - 1: `i + size + 2` passed Int32's end and
      # panicked.
      return nil if size > n - i - 2
      i = i + size
      return "a chunk is not followed by CRLF" unless p[i] == 13_u8 && p[i + 1] == 10_u8
      i = i + 2
    end
  end

  # Without the blanks at either end (RFC 9112 §5.1: field values are
  # trimmed of the whitespace around them).
  def self.trim(s : String) : String
    p = s.to_unsafe
    first = 0
    last = s.bytesize
    while first < last && (p[first] == 32_u8 || p[first] == 9_u8)
      first = first + 1
    end
    while last > first && (p[last - 1] == 32_u8 || p[last - 1] == 9_u8)
      last = last - 1
    end
    s[first, last - first]
  end
end

# A `multipart/form-data` body (RFC 7578) read a part at a time, as it
# arrives: `next_part` answers the next `Part`, nil after the last, and a
# part's `read` answers its bytes up to the boundary, "" at its end. A part
# not read to its end is read past by the next `next_part`. The body of a
# request is `Request#multipart`'s; any `BodyReader` and boundary will do.
# A part's head is refused past 16 KiB, and a body that ends before its
# closing boundary is an `HTTPError`.
pub class Multipart
  @body : BodyReader
  # `\r\n--` and the boundary: what ends a part.
  @delimiter : String
  @held : String
  @started : Bool
  @done : Bool
  @current : Part?
  # Whether the part being read has met its boundary, which is consumed
  # with the part's last bytes.
  @part_done : Bool

  HEAD_LIMIT = 16384

  def initialize(@body : BodyReader, boundary : String)
    raise "HTTP: a multipart boundary is 1 to 70 characters, not #{boundary.bytesize}" if boundary.bytesize < 1 || boundary.bytesize > 70
    @delimiter = "\r\n--" + boundary
    # A body starts with its first boundary without a CRLF before it; one
    # is put there, so every boundary is found the same way.
    @held = "\r\n"
    @started = false
    @done = false
    @current = nil
    @part_done = false
  end

  # The boundary a `Content-Type` of `multipart/...` names, nil when it
  # names none - or one of a length RFC 2046 §5.1.1 does not allow, 1 to
  # 70 characters, which `new` refuses with a panic. The type is the
  # peer's: `Request#multipart` handed a client's 90-character boundary
  # through to that panic (found by bench/std_fuzz_exercise.sh).
  def self.boundary(content_type : String) : String?
    parsed = MIME.parse?(content_type)
    return nil unless parsed
    media, params = parsed
    return nil unless media.starts_with?("multipart/")
    found = params["boundary"]?
    return nil unless found && found.bytesize >= 1 && found.bytesize <= 70
    found
  end

  # The next part, nil after the last.
  def next_part : Part | Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    return nil if @done
    if part = @current
      until part.finished?
        got = part.read(65536)
        return got unless got.is_a?(String)
      end
      @current = nil
    else
      # Past the preamble to the first boundary.
      until @started
        found = find(@delimiter)
        if found >= 0
          @held = @held[found + @delimiter.bytesize, @held.bytesize - found - @delimiter.bytesize]
          @started = true
        else
          keep = @delimiter.bytesize - 1
          @held = @held[@held.bytesize - keep, keep] if @held.bytesize > keep
          more = pull
          return more unless more.is_a?(Bool)
          return HTTPError.new("multipart body ends before its first boundary") unless more
        end
      end
    end
    # After a boundary: `--` ends the body, CRLF begins a part's head.
    while @held.bytesize < 2
      more = pull
      return more unless more.is_a?(Bool)
      return HTTPError.new("multipart body ends after a boundary") unless more
    end
    if @held.starts_with?("--")
      @done = true
      return nil
    end
    # Transport padding (spaces and tabs) may stand before the CRLF.
    skip = 0
    while true
      while skip >= @held.bytesize
        more = pull
        return more unless more.is_a?(Bool)
        return HTTPError.new("multipart body ends after a boundary") unless more
      end
      c = @held.to_unsafe[skip]
      break unless c == 32_u8 || c == 9_u8
      skip = skip + 1
    end
    @held = @held[skip, @held.bytesize - skip] if skip > 0
    while @held.bytesize < 2
      more = pull
      return more unless more.is_a?(Bool)
      return HTTPError.new("multipart body ends after a boundary") unless more
    end
    return HTTPError.new("a multipart boundary is not followed by CRLF") unless @held.starts_with?("\r\n")
    @held = @held[2, @held.bytesize - 2]
    head_end = find("\r\n\r\n")
    while head_end < 0
      return HTTPError.new("a multipart part's head past #{HEAD_LIMIT} bytes") if @held.bytesize > HEAD_LIMIT
      more = pull
      return more unless more.is_a?(Bool)
      unless more
        # A part with no fields at all: its blank line is the head's end.
        return HTTPError.new("multipart body ends inside a part's head") unless @held.starts_with?("\r\n")
        break
      end
      head_end = find("\r\n\r\n")
    end
    headers = {} of String => String
    if @held.starts_with?("\r\n")
      @held = @held[2, @held.bytesize - 2]
    else
      lines = HTTP.split_crlf("\r\n" + @held[0, head_end])
      fields = HTTP.parse_fields(lines)
      return HTTPError.new("a multipart part's head: #{fields}") if fields.is_a?(String)
      headers = fields
      @held = @held[head_end + 4, @held.bytesize - head_end - 4]
    end
    part = Part.new(self, headers)
    @current = part
    @part_done = false
    part
  end

  # Where *pattern* is in what is held, -1 when it is not there yet.
  protected def find(pattern : String) : Int32
    @held.byte_index(pattern) || -1
  end

  # One more piece of the body onto what is held: false at its end.
  protected def pull : Bool | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    got = @body.read(65536)
    return got unless got.is_a?(String)
    return false if got.bytesize == 0
    @held = @held + got
    true
  end

  # Up to *max* bytes of the part being read, "" at its boundary. What is
  # held back is what may yet be the start of the boundary.
  protected def part_read(max : Int32) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    return "" if @part_done
    while true
      if at = @held.byte_index(@delimiter)
        take = at < max ? at : max
        piece = @held[0, take]
        @held = @held[take, @held.bytesize - take]
        if take == at
          @held = @held[@delimiter.bytesize, @held.bytesize - @delimiter.bytesize]
          @part_done = true
        end
        return piece
      end
      safe = @held.bytesize - (@delimiter.bytesize - 1)
      if safe > 0
        take = safe < max ? safe : max
        piece = @held[0, take]
        @held = @held[take, @held.bytesize - take]
        return piece
      end
      more = pull
      return more unless more.is_a?(Bool)
      return HTTPError.new("multipart body ends inside a part") unless more
    end
  end
end

# One part of a multipart body: its head's fields, its name and file name
# from `Content-Disposition`, and its bytes, read up to its boundary.
pub class Part
  getter headers : Hash(String, String)
  @owner : Multipart
  @finished : Bool
  @pending : String?

  def initialize(@owner : Multipart, @headers : Hash(String, String))
    @finished = false
    @pending = nil
  end

  def header(name : String) : String?
    @headers.each do |key, value|
      return value if HTTP.same_name?(key, name)
    end
    nil
  end

  # The form field's name, `name=` of `Content-Disposition`.
  def name : String?
    disposition("name")
  end

  # The file name a browser sent the part under, `filename=`.
  def filename : String?
    disposition("filename")
  end

  def content_type : String?
    header("Content-Type")
  end

  def finished? : Bool
    @finished
  end

  # The part's next bytes, at most *max*; "" at its end.
  def read(max : Int32 = 65536) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    return "" if @finished
    got = @owner.part_read(max)
    @finished = true if got.is_a?(String) && got.bytesize == 0
    got
  end

  # The rest of the part as one string, refused past *limit* bytes.
  def read_all(limit : Int64 = 2147483647_i64) : String | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    text = String::Builder.new
    total = 0_i64
    while true
      got = read(65536)
      return got unless got.is_a?(String)
      break if got.bytesize == 0
      total = total + got.bytesize.to_i64
      return HTTPError.new("a multipart part past #{limit} bytes", 413) if total > limit
      text << got
    end
    text.to_s
  end

  private def disposition(key : String) : String?
    value = header("Content-Disposition")
    return nil unless value
    parsed = MIME.parse?("x/" + value)
    if parsed
      return parsed[1][key]?
    end
    nil
  end
end

# A multipart/form-data body to send: fields and files in order. Its
# boundary is chosen when the body is first asked for - `finish`,
# `content_type` or `boundary`, whichever comes first, after which no
# part is added - as `iyi-` and 32 hex digits of the parts' SHA-1, made
# again from itself in the rare case a part holds it: so it is none of
# the parts' bytes by construction rather than by chance, the same
# fields make the same body, and std/http reaches no source of
# randomness for it. A boundary given to `new` is used as it is, and a
# part that holds it is refused.
pub class MultipartBuilder
  @boundary : String?
  @parts : Array({String, String?, String?, String})

  def initialize(boundary : String? = nil)
    if given = boundary
      raise "HTTP: a multipart boundary is 1 to 70 characters, not #{given.bytesize}" if given.bytesize < 1 || given.bytesize > 70
    end
    @boundary = boundary
    @parts = [] of {String, String?, String?, String}
  end

  def boundary : String
    if chosen = @boundary
      return chosen
    end
    digest = SHA1.new
    @parts.each do |part|
      digest.update(part[0])
      digest.update(part[1] || "")
      digest.update(part[2] || "")
      digest.update(part[3])
    end
    chosen = "iyi-" + digest.hexfinal[0, 32]
    while @parts.any? { |part| MultipartBuilder.holds?(part[3], chosen) }
      chosen = "iyi-" + SHA1.hexdigest(chosen)[0, 32]
    end
    @boundary = chosen
    chosen
  end

  def content_type : String
    "multipart/form-data; boundary=" + boundary
  end

  def field(name : String, value : String) : self
    add(name, nil, nil, value)
  end

  def file(name : String, filename : String, data : String, content_type : String = MIME.from_extension(filename)) : self
    add(name, filename, content_type, data)
  end

  # The body, closing boundary and all.
  def finish : String
    mark = boundary
    text = String::Builder.new
    @parts.each do |part|
      text << "--" << mark << "\r\n"
      text << "Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"" << MultipartBuilder.quoted(part[0]) << "\""
      if file = part[1]
        text << "; filename=\"" << MultipartBuilder.quoted(file) << "\""
      end
      text << "\r\n"
      if kind = part[2]
        text << "Content-Type: " << kind << "\r\n"
      end
      text << "\r\n" << part[3] << "\r\n"
    end
    text << "--" << mark << "--\r\n"
    text.to_s
  end

  private def add(name : String, filename : String?, type : String?, data : String) : self
    if mark = @boundary
      raise "HTTP: a multipart part's data holds its boundary" if MultipartBuilder.holds?(data, mark)
    end
    if kind = type
      raise "HTTP: a part's Content-Type contains a line break" if HTTP.has_break?(kind)
    end
    @parts << {name, filename, type, data}
    self
  end

  # The boundary ends a part where it follows a CRLF, the part's first
  # bytes included, which follow the head's blank line.
  protected def self.holds?(data : String, mark : String) : Bool
    ("\r\n" + data).includes?("\r\n--" + mark)
  end

  # A name inside quotes, as browsers write one: a quote and a line break
  # percent-encoded (the WHATWG form encoding), so no field can end the
  # parameter or the line early.
  protected def self.quoted(text : String) : String
    built = String::Builder.new
    text.each_char do |c|
      if c == '"'
        built << "%22"
      elsif c == '\r'
        built << "%0D"
      elsif c == '\n'
        built << "%0A"
      else
        built << c
      end
    end
    built.to_s
  end
end

# -- HTTP/2 (RFC 9113) -------------------------------------------------------
#
# The same requests and answers, as frames over one connection. A server's
# connection whose first bytes are HTTP/2's preface is served this way
# (h2c, the prior-knowledge start of RFC 9113 §3.3); a client speaks it
# where TLS's ALPN chose `h2` - offered when a program lists it in
# `HTTP.tls_config.alpn` - and, over `http://`, from an
# `HTTPClient.new(prior_knowledge: true)`.
#
# One fiber runs a connection. It reads frames, and whatever must wait - a
# body's next piece, a window to send in, the next request - reads frames
# until it can go on, keeping what completes for other streams meanwhile:
# no frame is ever written by two writers, and no lock is taken. A
# server's block runs for one stream at a time, as for one HTTP/1.1
# connection; a client sends one request at a time over its connection.
#
# Flow control: the connection's window is opened again as DATA arrives,
# and a stream's as its body is read, so a body nobody reads holds its
# sender at the stream's window rather than filling this process. Server
# push is not offered (SETTINGS_ENABLE_PUSH 0) and a PUSH_PROMISE is the
# error it then is; priority signals are read and ignored (§5.3). A header
# block - HEADERS and its CONTINUATIONs - past the limit on a head is
# refused as it arrives, not after it has been held whole.
class H2Stream
  getter id : Int32
  # The head, decoded: a request's, or an answer's final one.
  property fields : Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField)? = nil
  # DATA arrived and not yet read, and the body's bytes so far.
  property pieces : Array(String) = [] of String
  property received : Int64 = 0_i64
  # A `content-length` the head gave, -1 for none.
  property declared : Int64 = -1_i64
  # The peer's END_STREAM; a RST_STREAM's code (either side's), -1 for none.
  property ended : Bool = false
  property reset : Int32 = -1
  property send_window : Int64
  property recv_window : Int64
  # This end's END_STREAM went out.
  property sent_end : Bool = false
  # A server's request past its limit on a body: answered 413.
  property too_large : Bool = false

  def initialize(@id : Int32, @send_window : Int64, @recv_window : Int64)
  end
end

class H2Connection
  DATA          = 0
  HEADERS       = 1
  PRIORITY      = 2
  RST_STREAM    = 3
  SETTINGS      = 4
  PUSH_PROMISE  = 5
  PING          = 6
  GOAWAY        = 7
  WINDOW_UPDATE = 8
  CONTINUATION  = 9

  END_STREAM    =    1
  ACK           =    1
  END_HEADERS   =    4
  PADDED        =    8
  PRIORITY_FLAG = 0x20

  NO_ERROR           =  0
  PROTOCOL_ERROR     =  1
  INTERNAL_ERROR     =  2
  FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR =  3
  STREAM_CLOSED      =  5
  FRAME_SIZE_ERROR   =  6
  REFUSED_STREAM     =  7
  CANCEL             =  8
  COMPRESSION_ERROR  =  9
  ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM  = 11
  HTTP_1_1_REQUIRED  = 13

  HEAD_BLOCK    = 0
  TRAILER_BLOCK = 1
  IGNORED_BLOCK = 2
  CLOSED_BLOCK  = 3
  REFUSED_BLOCK = 4
  # A head on a stream long closed: decoded, then a connection error.
  STALE_BLOCK = 5

  PREFACE = "PRI * HTTP/2.0\r\n\r\nSM\r\n\r\n"
  # The windows and the frame size every connection starts with (§6.5.2,
  # §6.9.2), and the largest window there is.
  WINDOW     =      65535_i64
  FRAME      =          16384
  MAX_WINDOW = 2147483647_i64
  # What this end takes: streams open at once, and a head's fields - their
  # names, values and 32 octets each (§6.5.2), the same limit as a
  # HTTP/1.1 head's bytes.
  STREAMS     =     100
  HEADER_LIST = 1048576

  getter conn : Conn
  getter failure : HTTPError | Cancelled | SocketError | Nil
  @server : Bool
  @streams : Hash(Int32, H2Stream)
  @send_window : Int64
  @recv_window : Int64
  @peer_window : Int64
  @peer_frame : Int32
  @peer_streams : Int64
  @encoder : Std::Hpack::Encoder
  @decoder : Std::Hpack::Decoder
  # The highest stream the peer opened (a server's), the next this end
  # opens (a client's).
  @highest : Int32
  @next_id : Int32
  # A header block still open: its stream, its bytes, its END_STREAM, and
  # what it is - a head, trailers, or a block decoded only to keep the
  # connection's HPACK table (a stream reset or closed, or refused).
  @block_stream : Int32
  @block : Array(UInt8)
  @block_end : Bool
  @block_mode : Int32
  # The stream a server's block is answering, kept while it does.
  @current : Int32
  # Streams this end reset lately: frames the peer sent before it heard
  # are dropped (§5.4.2), where on another closed stream they are errors.
  @reset_lately : Array(Int32)
  # Frames written and not yet sent: sent together before any read that
  # may wait, and once they pass PENDING - a frame a write had Nagle hold
  # each small one back for the last one's ACK, 40 ms a request.
  @pending : Array(String)
  @pending_size : Int32

  PENDING = 65536
  # A server's streams whose head has come, in order.
  @ready : Array(Int32)
  # The last stream the peer's GOAWAY said it would answer, -1 for none.
  @goaway : Int32
  @settled : Bool
  # A request's body is refused past it (a server's).
  @limit : Int64

  def initialize(@conn : Conn, @server : Bool, @limit : Int64 = 9223372036854775807_i64)
    @streams = Hash(Int32, H2Stream).new
    @send_window = WINDOW
    @recv_window = WINDOW
    @peer_window = WINDOW
    @peer_frame = FRAME
    @peer_streams = 2147483647_i64
    @encoder = Std::Hpack::Encoder.new
    @decoder = Std::Hpack::Decoder.new(max_header_list_size: HEADER_LIST)
    @highest = 0
    @next_id = 1
    @block_stream = 0
    @block = [] of UInt8
    @block_end = false
    @block_mode = HEAD_BLOCK
    @current = 0
    @reset_lately = [] of Int32
    @pending = [] of String
    @pending_size = 0
    @ready = [] of Int32
    @goaway = -1
    @settled = false
    @failure = nil
  end

  # This end's first frames: the client's preface, then SETTINGS - push
  # off, and the streams a server takes at once.
  def start : Bool
    settings = @server ? H2Connection.setting(3, STREAMS) + H2Connection.setting(6, HEADER_LIST) : H2Connection.setting(2, 0) + H2Connection.setting(6, HEADER_LIST)
    unless @server
      @pending << PREFACE
      @pending_size = @pending_size + PREFACE.bytesize
    end
    write_frame(SETTINGS, 0, 0, settings) && flush
  end

  def stream(id : Int32) : H2Stream?
    @streams[id]?
  end

  # Whether a new request may go on this connection: it has not failed,
  # the server has not said it is going away, and stream numbers remain.
  def usable? : Bool
    @failure.nil? && @goaway < 0 && @next_id < 2147483000 && !@conn.ended?
  end

  # Whether the last request's stream was refused unprocessed (§8.7):
  # REFUSED_STREAM, or past the last stream a GOAWAY said it would answer.
  @refused : Bool = false

  def refused? : Bool
    @refused
  end

  # -- reading

  # One frame read and acted on. False once the connection is over: the
  # peer closed it, it failed (`failure`), or it broke the protocol, which
  # has been answered with a GOAWAY that says how.
  def read_frame : Bool
    return false unless @failure.nil?
    return false unless flush
    return ended unless @conn.need(9)
    length = (@conn.peek(0).to_i32 << 16) | (@conn.peek(1).to_i32 << 8) | @conn.peek(2).to_i32
    type = @conn.peek(3).to_i32
    flags = @conn.peek(4).to_i32
    id = ((@conn.peek(5).to_i32 & 0x7F) << 24) | (@conn.peek(6).to_i32 << 16) | (@conn.peek(7).to_i32 << 8) | @conn.peek(8).to_i32
    return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "a frame of #{length} bytes, past the #{FRAME} this end takes") if length > FRAME
    return ended unless @conn.need(9 + length)
    @conn.consume(9)
    payload = @conn.take(length)
    if @block_stream != 0 && (type != CONTINUATION || id != @block_stream)
      return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "a header block broken off by another frame")
    end
    if !@settled && (type != SETTINGS || flags & ACK != 0)
      return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "the connection does not begin with SETTINGS")
    end
    case type
    when DATA          then on_data(id, flags, payload)
    when HEADERS       then on_headers(id, flags, payload)
    when PRIORITY      then on_priority(id, payload)
    when RST_STREAM    then on_reset(id, payload)
    when SETTINGS      then on_settings(id, flags, payload)
    when PUSH_PROMISE  then fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "a PUSH_PROMISE, where push was not offered")
    when PING          then on_ping(id, flags, payload)
    when GOAWAY        then on_goaway(id, payload)
    when WINDOW_UPDATE then on_window(id, payload)
    when CONTINUATION  then on_continuation(id, flags, payload)
    else                    true
    end
  end

  # The peer closed, or the read failed.
  private def ended : Bool
    if fault = @conn.failure
      @failure = fault
    elsif @conn.available > 0 || @block_stream != 0
      @failure = HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the connection ended inside a frame")
    end
    false
  end

  # A connection error (§5.4.1): GOAWAY with its code and why, and no more.
  private def fail(code : Int32, why : String) : Bool
    if @failure.nil?
      write_frame(GOAWAY, 0, 0, H2Connection.u32(@highest) + H2Connection.u32(code) + why)
      flush
    end
    @failure = HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: #{why} (#{H2Connection.error_name(code)})")
    false
  end

  # A stream error (§5.4.2): RST_STREAM, and the stream is over.
  private def refuse(id : Int32, code : Int32) : Bool
    if s = @streams[id]?
      s.reset = code
      s.ended = true
      s.pieces.clear
    end
    # A server forgets the stream, unless its block is answering it and
    # finds out from the stream itself.
    @streams.delete(id) if @server && id != @current
    reset_lately(id)
    write_frame(RST_STREAM, 0, id, H2Connection.u32(code))
  end

  private def reset_lately(id : Int32) : Nil
    @reset_lately << id
    @reset_lately.shift if @reset_lately.size > 64
  end

  # A stream the peer may not use yet: one it has not opened, or one this
  # end has not.
  private def idle?(id : Int32) : Bool
    if @server
      id % 2 == 0 || id > @highest
    else
      id % 2 == 0 || id >= @next_id
    end
  end

  # The payload without its padding (§6.1), or nil when the padding claims
  # more than the frame holds.
  private def unpadded(flags : Int32, payload : String) : String?
    return payload if flags & PADDED == 0
    return nil if payload.bytesize < 1
    pad = payload.to_unsafe[0].to_i32
    return nil if pad >= payload.bytesize
    payload[1, payload.bytesize - 1 - pad]
  end

  private def on_data(id : Int32, flags : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "DATA on stream 0") if id == 0
    body = unpadded(flags, payload)
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "DATA whose padding is longer than the frame") if body.nil?
    size = payload.bytesize.to_i64
    return fail(FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR, "DATA past the connection's window") if size > @recv_window
    # The connection's window opens again at once: what holds a sender
    # back is the stream's.
    if size > 0_i64
      return false unless write_frame(WINDOW_UPDATE, 0, 0, H2Connection.u32(size.to_i32))
    end
    s = @streams[id]?
    if s.nil?
      return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "DATA on a stream never opened") if idle?(id)
      # A stream this end reset: what was in flight is dropped (§5.4.2);
      # on any other closed stream DATA is an error (§5.1).
      return true if @reset_lately.includes?(id)
      return refuse(id, STREAM_CLOSED)
    end
    return true if s.reset >= 0
    return refuse(id, STREAM_CLOSED) if s.ended
    return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if s.fields.nil?
    return refuse(id, FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR) if size > s.recv_window
    s.recv_window = s.recv_window - size
    # Padding is nobody's to read: its share of the window comes back now.
    padding = size - body.bytesize.to_i64
    if padding > 0_i64 && flags & END_STREAM == 0
      s.recv_window = s.recv_window + padding
      return false unless write_frame(WINDOW_UPDATE, 0, id, H2Connection.u32(padding.to_i32))
    end
    s.received = s.received + body.bytesize.to_i64
    return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if s.declared >= 0_i64 && s.received > s.declared
    if @server && s.received > @limit
      s.too_large = true
      s.pieces.clear
    elsif body.bytesize > 0
      s.pieces << body
    end
    if flags & END_STREAM != 0
      s.ended = true
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if s.declared >= 0_i64 && s.received != s.declared
    end
    true
  end

  private def on_headers(id : Int32, flags : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "HEADERS on stream 0") if id == 0
    block = unpadded(flags, payload)
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "HEADERS whose padding is longer than the frame") if block.nil?
    if flags & PRIORITY_FLAG != 0
      return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "HEADERS too short for the priority it says it has") if block.bytesize < 5
      p = block.to_unsafe
      depends = ((p[0].to_i32 & 0x7F) << 24) | (p[1].to_i32 << 16) | (p[2].to_i32 << 8) | p[3].to_i32
      block = block[5, block.bytesize - 5]
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if depends == id
    end
    s = @streams[id]?
    mode = HEAD_BLOCK
    if s.nil?
      if @server
        return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "a stream opened with the even number #{id}") if id % 2 == 0
        if id > @highest
          @highest = id
          s = H2Stream.new(id, @peer_window, WINDOW)
          @streams[id] = s
          # Past the streams a server takes at once, the head is still read -
          # the HPACK table is the connection's - and the stream refused.
          mode = REFUSED_BLOCK if open_count > STREAMS
        else
          mode = @reset_lately.includes?(id) ? IGNORED_BLOCK : STALE_BLOCK
        end
      else
        return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "HEADERS on a stream this client did not open") if idle?(id)
        mode = @reset_lately.includes?(id) ? IGNORED_BLOCK : STALE_BLOCK
      end
    elsif s.reset >= 0
      mode = IGNORED_BLOCK
    elsif s.ended
      mode = CLOSED_BLOCK
    elsif s.fields
      mode = TRAILER_BLOCK
    end
    @block_stream = id
    @block = [] of UInt8
    @block_end = flags & END_STREAM != 0
    @block_mode = mode
    return false unless gather(block)
    flags & END_HEADERS != 0 ? close_block : true
  end

  private def on_continuation(id : Int32, flags : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "CONTINUATION with no header block open") if @block_stream == 0
    return false unless gather(payload)
    flags & END_HEADERS != 0 ? close_block : true
  end

  # A piece of the open header block, refused past what a head may hold:
  # CONTINUATION after CONTINUATION is no way to make this end keep more.
  private def gather(piece : String) : Bool
    return fail(ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM, "a header block past #{HEADER_LIST} bytes") if @block.size + piece.bytesize > HEADER_LIST
    p = piece.to_unsafe
    i = 0
    while i < piece.bytesize
      @block << p[i]
      i = i + 1
    end
    true
  end

  private def close_block : Bool
    id = @block_stream
    @block_stream = 0
    bytes = Std::Hpack::Bytes.new(@block.size) { |i| @block[i] }
    @block = [] of UInt8
    decoded = @decoder.decode(bytes)
    return fail(COMPRESSION_ERROR, "a header block that does not decode: #{decoded.message}") if decoded.is_a?(Std::Hpack::HpackError)
    s = @streams[id]?
    case @block_mode
    when IGNORED_BLOCK
      return true
    when CLOSED_BLOCK
      return refuse(id, STREAM_CLOSED)
    when REFUSED_BLOCK
      return refuse(id, REFUSED_STREAM)
    when STALE_BLOCK
      return fail(STREAM_CLOSED, "HEADERS on stream #{id}, which is closed")
    end
    return true if s.nil?
    if @block_mode == TRAILER_BLOCK
      # Trailers: the end of the stream, and no pseudo-header in them.
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) unless @block_end
      decoded.each { |f| return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if f.name.starts_with?(":") }
      s.ended = true
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if @server && s.declared >= 0_i64 && s.received != s.declared
      return true
    end
    if @server
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if H2Connection.request_flaw(decoded)
    else
      status = H2Connection.answer_status(decoded)
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if status.nil?
      # An interim answer: the final one is still to come.
      if status < 200
        return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if @block_end
        return true
      end
    end
    s.fields = decoded
    if declared = H2Connection.field(decoded, "content-length")
      n = HTTP.content_length(declared)
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) unless n
      s.declared = n
    end
    if @block_end
      s.ended = true
      # A request that ends with its head has no body to have a length.
      return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if @server && s.declared > 0_i64
    end
    @ready << id if @server
    true
  end

  private def open_count : Int32
    n = 0
    @streams.each { |_, s| n = n + 1 if s.reset < 0 && !(s.ended && s.sent_end) }
    n
  end

  private def on_priority(id : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "PRIORITY on stream 0") if id == 0
    return refuse(id, FRAME_SIZE_ERROR) if payload.bytesize != 5
    p = payload.to_unsafe
    depends = ((p[0].to_i32 & 0x7F) << 24) | (p[1].to_i32 << 16) | (p[2].to_i32 << 8) | p[3].to_i32
    return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if depends == id
    true
  end

  private def on_reset(id : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "RST_STREAM on stream 0") if id == 0
    return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "RST_STREAM of #{payload.bytesize} bytes") if payload.bytesize != 4
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "RST_STREAM on a stream never opened") if idle?(id) && @streams[id]?.nil?
    if s = @streams[id]?
      s.reset = H2Connection.read_u32(payload, 0)
      s.ended = true
      s.pieces.clear
    end
    true
  end

  private def on_settings(id : Int32, flags : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "SETTINGS on stream #{id}") if id != 0
    if flags & ACK != 0
      return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "a SETTINGS acknowledgement that carries #{payload.bytesize} bytes") if payload.bytesize != 0
      return true
    end
    return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "SETTINGS of #{payload.bytesize} bytes, not a multiple of 6") if payload.bytesize % 6 != 0
    at = 0
    while at < payload.bytesize
      p = payload.to_unsafe
      name = (p[at].to_i32 << 8) | p[at + 1].to_i32
      value = H2Connection.read_u32(payload, at + 2).to_i64 & 0xFFFFFFFF_i64
      case name
      when 1
        @encoder.protocol_max_table_size = value > 2147483647_i64 ? 2147483647 : value.to_i32
      when 2
        return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "SETTINGS_ENABLE_PUSH of #{value}") if value > 1_i64
        return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "a server asking for push") if !@server && value == 1_i64
      when 3
        @peer_streams = value
      when 4
        return fail(FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR, "SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE of #{value}, past 2^31-1") if value > MAX_WINDOW
        delta = value - @peer_window
        @streams.each do |_, s|
          s.send_window = s.send_window + delta
          return fail(FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR, "a stream's window past 2^31-1") if s.send_window > MAX_WINDOW
        end
        @peer_window = value
      when 5
        return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE of #{value}") if value < 16384_i64 || value > 16777215_i64
        @peer_frame = value.to_i32
      end
      at = at + 6
    end
    @settled = true
    write_frame(SETTINGS, ACK, 0, "")
  end

  private def on_ping(id : Int32, flags : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "PING on stream #{id}") if id != 0
    return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "PING of #{payload.bytesize} bytes") if payload.bytesize != 8
    return true if flags & ACK != 0
    write_frame(PING, ACK, 0, payload)
  end

  private def on_goaway(id : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "GOAWAY on stream #{id}") if id != 0
    return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "GOAWAY of #{payload.bytesize} bytes") if payload.bytesize < 8
    @goaway = H2Connection.read_u32(payload, 0) & 0x7FFFFFFF
    # What the peer will not answer is refused, and may be sent again
    # on another connection (§6.8).
    @streams.each do |sid, s|
      if sid > @goaway && s.reset < 0 && !s.ended
        s.reset = REFUSED_STREAM
        s.ended = true
      end
    end
    true
  end

  private def on_window(id : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return fail(FRAME_SIZE_ERROR, "WINDOW_UPDATE of #{payload.bytesize} bytes") if payload.bytesize != 4
    increment = (H2Connection.read_u32(payload, 0) & 0x7FFFFFFF).to_i64
    if id == 0
      return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "a WINDOW_UPDATE of 0 for the connection") if increment == 0_i64
      @send_window = @send_window + increment
      return fail(FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR, "the connection's window past 2^31-1") if @send_window > MAX_WINDOW
      return true
    end
    s = @streams[id]?
    if s.nil?
      return fail(PROTOCOL_ERROR, "WINDOW_UPDATE on a stream never opened") if idle?(id)
      return true
    end
    return refuse(id, PROTOCOL_ERROR) if increment == 0_i64
    s.send_window = s.send_window + increment
    return refuse(id, FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR) if s.send_window > MAX_WINDOW
    true
  end

  # -- writing

  # One frame, in one write.
  def write_frame(type : Int32, flags : Int32, id : Int32, payload : String) : Bool
    return false unless @failure.nil? || (type == GOAWAY && @failure.is_a?(HTTPError))
    n = payload.bytesize
    head = String.new(9) do |b|
      b[0] = ((n >> 16) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[1] = ((n >> 8) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[2] = (n & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[3] = (type & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[4] = (flags & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[5] = (((id >> 24) & 0x7F) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[6] = ((id >> 16) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[7] = ((id >> 8) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[8] = (id & 0xFF).to_u8
      nil
    end
    @pending << head
    @pending << payload if n > 0
    @pending_size = @pending_size + 9 + n
    @pending_size >= PENDING ? flush : true
  end

  # What has been written, sent in one write.
  def flush : Bool
    return @failure.nil? if @pending.empty?
    whole = @pending.size == 1 ? @pending[0] : @pending.join("")
    @pending.clear
    @pending_size = 0
    got = @conn.transport.write(whole)
    return true if got.is_a?(Int32)
    @failure = got if @failure.nil?
    false
  end

  # A head: HEADERS, and CONTINUATION after it for what one frame of the
  # peer's size does not hold.
  def send_headers(id : Int32, fields : Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField), end_stream : Bool) : Bool
    encoded = @encoder.encode(fields)
    block = String.new(encoded.size) do |b|
      b.copy_from(encoded.to_unsafe, encoded.size)
      nil
    end
    at = 0
    first = true
    while first || at < block.bytesize
      take = block.bytesize - at
      take = @peer_frame if take > @peer_frame
      last = at + take == block.bytesize
      flags = last ? END_HEADERS : 0
      if first
        flags = flags | END_STREAM if end_stream
        return false unless write_frame(HEADERS, flags, id, block[at, take])
      else
        return false unless write_frame(CONTINUATION, flags, id, block[at, take])
      end
      at = at + take
      first = false
    end
    if end_stream
      if s = @streams[id]?
        s.sent_end = true
      end
    end
    true
  end

  # *text* on stream *id* as the windows allow, frames read while they are
  # shut. False when the stream or the connection ends first.
  def send_data(id : Int32, text : String, end_stream : Bool) : Bool
    s = @streams[id]?
    return false if s.nil?
    at = 0
    while at < text.bytesize
      return false if s.reset >= 0 || !@failure.nil?
      room = text.bytesize - at
      room = @peer_frame if room > @peer_frame
      room = @send_window.to_i32 if @send_window < room.to_i64
      room = s.send_window.to_i32 if s.send_window < room.to_i64
      if room <= 0
        return false unless read_frame
        next
      end
      last = end_stream && at + room == text.bytesize
      @send_window = @send_window - room.to_i64
      s.send_window = s.send_window - room.to_i64
      return false unless write_frame(DATA, last ? END_STREAM : 0, id, text[at, room])
      at = at + room
      s.sent_end = true if last
    end
    if end_stream && !s.sent_end
      return false if s.reset >= 0
      return false unless write_frame(DATA, END_STREAM, id, "")
      s.sent_end = true
    end
    true
  end

  # The next piece of stream *id*'s body, "" at its end; frames are read
  # until one comes. What is taken opens the stream's window again.
  def body_piece(id : Int32, max : Int32) : String | HTTPError | Cancelled | SocketError
    while true
      s = @streams[id]?
      return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: stream #{id} is gone") if s.nil?
      return HTTPError.new("body past #{@limit} bytes", 413) if s.too_large
      if s.pieces.size > 0
        piece = s.pieces[0]
        if piece.bytesize > max
          s.pieces[0] = piece[max, piece.bytesize - max]
          piece = piece[0, max]
        else
          s.pieces.shift
        end
        unless s.ended
          s.recv_window = s.recv_window + piece.bytesize.to_i64
          write_frame(WINDOW_UPDATE, 0, id, H2Connection.u32(piece.bytesize))
        end
        return piece
      end
      if s.reset >= 0
        return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the stream was reset (#{H2Connection.error_name(s.reset)})")
      end
      return "" if s.ended
      unless read_frame
        if fault = @failure
          return fault
        end
        return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the connection ended inside a body")
      end
    end
  end

  # -- a server's side

  # The next stream whose request's head has come: frames are read until
  # one has. Nil at the connection's end.
  def next_request : Int32?
    while true
      while id = @ready.shift?
        s = @streams[id]?
        if s && s.reset < 0
          @current = id
          return id
        end
        @streams.delete(id)
      end
      return nil unless read_frame
    end
  end

  # The request stream *id* carries, its body to be read as it comes; nil
  # when its head is not one (it has been reset already).
  def request(id : Int32, remote : String?) : Request?
    s = @streams[id]?
    return nil unless s
    fields = s.fields
    return nil unless fields
    method = ""
    target = ""
    authority = nil.as(String?)
    headers = {} of String => String
    fields.each do |f|
      case f.name
      when ":method"    then method = f.value
      when ":path"      then target = f.value
      when ":authority" then authority = f.value
      when ":scheme"    then nil
      else
        H2Connection.join(headers, f.name, f.value)
      end
    end
    if host = authority
      headers["host"] = host unless headers.has_key?("host")
    end
    path = target
    query = nil.as(String?)
    if mark = target.index('?')
      path = target[0, mark]
      query = target[mark + 1, target.bytesize - mark - 1]
    end
    reader = s.ended && s.pieces.empty? && !s.too_large ? BodyReader.new(nil, BodyReader::EMPTY, 0_i64, @limit) : BodyReader.frames(self, id, @limit)
    Request.new(method, path, query, headers, "HTTP/2", reader, remote)
  end

  # The block's answer to stream *id*: its head, then its body as the
  # windows allow. False when the connection is over.
  def answer(id : Int32, response : Response, req : Request, compress : Bool) : Bool
    s = @streams[id]?
    return @failure.nil? if s.nil? || s.reset >= 0
    # 101 has no meaning here (§8.6): the client is told to use HTTP/1.1.
    if response.takeover
      refuse(id, HTTP_1_1_REQUIRED)
      return @failure.nil?
    end
    head = HTTP.same_name?(req.method, "HEAD")
    bodiless = HTTP.bodiless?(response.status)
    coding = compress ? Server.coding_for(req, response) : nil
    # A stream's coding is gzip's, or none (`coding_for`).
    gzip = !coding.nil?
    if source = response.source
      defer source.close
      length = nil.as(Int64?)
      if declared = response.header("Content-Length")
        n = HTTP.content_length(declared)
        raise "HTTP: Content-Length #{declared.inspect} is not a number" unless n
        length = n
      end
      gzip = false if length
      shown = gzip ? Server.coded(response, "gzip") : response
      return false unless send_headers(id, H2Connection.answer_fields(shown, length, head), head || bodiless)
      return @failure.nil? if head || bodiless
      sink = H2Out.new(self, id)
      writer = gzip ? HTTP.gzip_writer(sink, false) : BodyWriter.new(sink, false)
      while true
        piece = source.read_bytes(16384)
        break if piece.bytesize == 0
        break unless writer.write(piece).nil?
      end
      writer.finish
      return finish_answer(s)
    end
    if block = response.writer
      shown = gzip ? Server.coded(response, "gzip") : response
      return false unless send_headers(id, H2Connection.answer_fields(shown, nil, head), head || bodiless)
      return @failure.nil? if head || bodiless
      sink = H2Out.new(self, id)
      writer = gzip ? HTTP.gzip_writer(sink, false) : BodyWriter.new(sink, false)
      block.call(writer)
      writer.finish
      return finish_answer(s)
    end
    response = Server.compressed(response, coding) if coding && response.body.bytesize >= Server::COMPRESS_FROM
    body = response.body
    empty = head || bodiless || body.bytesize == 0
    return false unless send_headers(id, H2Connection.answer_fields(response, bodiless ? nil : body.bytesize.to_i64, head), empty)
    return @failure.nil? if empty
    send_data(id, body, true)
    @failure.nil?
  end

  private def finish_answer(s : H2Stream) : Bool
    return @failure.nil? if s.reset >= 0
    send_data(s.id, "", true) unless s.sent_end
    @failure.nil?
  end

  # A stream the server is done with: a request body still coming is
  # stopped (§8.1, RST_STREAM NO_ERROR after a whole answer), and the
  # stream forgotten.
  def done(id : Int32) : Nil
    if s = @streams[id]?
      if !s.ended && s.reset < 0 && @failure.nil?
        reset_lately(id)
        write_frame(RST_STREAM, 0, id, H2Connection.u32(NO_ERROR))
      end
    end
    @streams.delete(id)
    @current = 0
  end

  # A server's refusal of a request it cannot serve: the status, and the
  # stream ended.
  def refuse_request(id : Int32, status : Int32, why : String) : Bool
    fields = [Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(":status", status.to_s), Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new("content-type", "text/plain"),
              Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new("content-length", (why.bytesize + 1).to_s)]
    return false unless send_headers(id, fields, false)
    send_data(id, why + "\n", true)
  end

  # GOAWAY: no new stream, the last one this end answered named.
  def goaway(code : Int32 = NO_ERROR) : Nil
    return unless @failure.nil?
    write_frame(GOAWAY, 0, 0, H2Connection.u32(@highest) + H2Connection.u32(code))
    flush
  end

  # -- a client's side

  # A request on a new stream: its head, and its body as the windows allow.
  def send_request(method : String, scheme : String, authority : String, path : String, headers : Hash(String, String), body : String | IyiIO) : Int32 | HTTPError | Cancelled | SocketError
    @refused = false
    id = @next_id
    @next_id = @next_id + 2
    s = H2Stream.new(id, @peer_window, WINDOW)
    @streams[id] = s
    fields = [Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(":method", method), Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(":scheme", scheme),
              Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(":authority", authority), Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(":path", path)]
    headers.each do |name, value|
      lower = name.downcase
      next if H2Connection.connection_specific?(lower) || lower == "host"
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a line break" if HTTP.has_break?(value)
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a control character" if HTTP.control?(value)
      fields << Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(lower, value, lower == "authorization" || lower == "proxy-authorization")
    end
    sending = true
    case body
    in String
      if body.bytesize > 0 || HTTP.same_name?(method, "POST") || HTTP.same_name?(method, "PUT") || HTTP.same_name?(method, "PATCH")
        given = false
        headers.each { |k, _| given = true if HTTP.same_name?(k, "content-length") }
        fields << Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new("content-length", body.bytesize.to_s) unless given
      end
      sending = send_headers(id, fields, body.bytesize == 0) && (body.bytesize == 0 || send_data(id, body, true))
    in IyiIO
      sending = send_headers(id, fields, false)
      while sending
        piece = body.read_bytes(16384)
        if piece.bytesize == 0
          sending = send_data(id, "", true)
          break
        end
        sending = send_data(id, piece, false)
      end
    end
    unless sending
      if fault = @failure
        return fault
      end
      if s.reset >= 0
        @refused = s.reset == REFUSED_STREAM
        return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the server reset the request (#{H2Connection.error_name(s.reset)})")
      end
      return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the request could not be sent")
    end
    id
  end

  # The final head of stream *id*'s answer: frames read until it comes.
  def answer_head(id : Int32) : Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField) | HTTPError | Cancelled | SocketError
    while true
      s = @streams[id]?
      return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: stream #{id} is gone") if s.nil?
      if fields = s.fields
        return fields
      end
      if s.reset >= 0
        @refused = s.reset == REFUSED_STREAM
        return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the server refused the request (REFUSED_STREAM)") if @refused
        return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the server reset the request (#{H2Connection.error_name(s.reset)})")
      end
      unless read_frame
        if fault = @failure
          return fault
        end
        return HTTPError.new("HTTP/2: the connection ended before an answer")
      end
    end
  end

  # The client is done with stream *id*: what is left of its answer is
  # cancelled, and the stream forgotten.
  def forget(id : Int32) : Nil
    if s = @streams[id]?
      if !s.ended && s.reset < 0 && @failure.nil?
        reset_lately(id)
        write_frame(RST_STREAM, 0, id, H2Connection.u32(CANCEL))
      end
    end
    @streams.delete(id)
  end

  # -- what a head is

  # Why a request's head is malformed (§8.1.1, §8.2, §8.3.1), or nil: its
  # pseudo-header fields - known, once each, before every other field, the
  # ones its method needs - and its fields - lower case, no connection
  # field, `te` only `trailers`, no value beginning or ending in white
  # space or holding NUL, CR or LF.
  def self.request_flaw(fields : Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField)) : String?
    method = nil.as(String?)
    path = nil.as(String?)
    scheme = nil.as(String?)
    authority = nil.as(String?)
    regular = false
    fields.each do |f|
      name = f.name
      if name.starts_with?(":")
        return "a pseudo-header after a field" if regular
        case name
        when ":method"
          return ":method twice" unless method.nil?
          method = f.value
        when ":path"
          return ":path twice" unless path.nil?
          path = f.value
        when ":scheme"
          return ":scheme twice" unless scheme.nil?
          scheme = f.value
        when ":authority"
          return ":authority twice" unless authority.nil?
          authority = f.value
        else
          return "the pseudo-header #{name}"
        end
      else
        regular = true
        flaw = field_flaw(f)
        return flaw if flaw
      end
    end
    return "no :method" if method.nil?
    if method == "CONNECT"
      return "CONNECT with :scheme or :path" unless scheme.nil? && path.nil?
      return "CONNECT without :authority" if authority.nil?
    else
      return "no :scheme" if scheme.nil?
      return "no :path, or an empty one" if path.nil? || path.empty?
    end
    nil
  end

  # An answer's final status, or nil when its head is malformed.
  def self.answer_status(fields : Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField)) : Int32?
    status = nil.as(Int32?)
    regular = false
    fields.each do |f|
      if f.name.starts_with?(":")
        return nil if regular || f.name != ":status" || !status.nil?
        v = f.value
        return nil unless v.bytesize == 3
        v.each_byte { |b| return nil unless b >= 48_u8 && b <= 57_u8 }
        status = v.to_i
      else
        regular = true
        return nil if field_flaw(f)
      end
    end
    status
  end

  def self.field_flaw(f : Std::Hpack::HeaderField) : String?
    name = f.name
    return "an empty field name" if name.empty?
    name.each_byte { |b| return "the field name #{name.inspect} is not lower case" if b >= 65_u8 && b <= 90_u8 }
    return "the field name #{name.inspect}" unless HTTP.token?(name)
    return "the connection-specific field #{name}" if connection_specific?(name)
    return "te other than trailers" if name == "te" && f.value != "trailers"
    v = f.value
    v.each_byte { |b| return "a value holding NUL, CR or LF" if b == 0_u8 || b == 13_u8 || b == 10_u8 }
    if v.bytesize > 0
      first = v.to_unsafe[0]
      last = v.to_unsafe[v.bytesize - 1]
      return "a value beginning or ending in white space" if first == 32_u8 || first == 9_u8 || last == 32_u8 || last == 9_u8
    end
    nil
  end

  def self.connection_specific?(name : String) : Bool
    name == "connection" || name == "keep-alive" || name == "proxy-connection" || name == "transfer-encoding" || name == "upgrade"
  end

  # An answer's fields: `:status` first, then each header on a field of its
  # own, its name in lower case; what only HTTP/1.1 means is dropped, and
  # the length written when the body's is known.
  def self.answer_fields(response : Response, length : Int64?, head : Bool) : Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField)
    raise "HTTP: not a status: #{response.status}" unless response.status >= 100 && response.status <= 999
    fields = [Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(":status", response.status.to_s)]
    has_length = false
    apart = response.apart
    response.headers.each do |name, value|
      raise "HTTP: not a header name: #{name.inspect}" unless HTTP.token?(name)
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a line break" if HTTP.has_break?(value)
      raise "HTTP: header #{name} contains a control character" if HTTP.control?(value)
      lower = name.downcase
      raise "HTTP: #{name} is not written; the body goes with its length" if lower == "transfer-encoding"
      next if connection_specific?(lower)
      if lower == "content-length"
        unless head || response.status == 304 || length.nil?
          n = HTTP.content_length(value)
          raise "HTTP: #{name} #{value.inspect} is not the body's #{length} bytes" unless n && n == length
        end
        has_length = true
      end
      lines = nil.as(Array(String)?)
      if split = apart
        lines = split[name]?
      end
      if each_line = lines
        each_line.each { |line| fields << Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(lower, H2Connection.trimmed(line)) }
      else
        fields << Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new(lower, H2Connection.trimmed(value))
      end
    end
    if !has_length && !HTTP.bodiless?(response.status)
      if written = length
        fields << Std::Hpack::HeaderField.new("content-length", written.to_s)
      end
    end
    fields
  end

  # A value with the white space at its ends gone, which HTTP/1.1 reads
  # past and HTTP/2 refuses.
  def self.trimmed(value : String) : String
    return value if value.empty?
    first = value.to_unsafe[0]
    last = value.to_unsafe[value.bytesize - 1]
    return value unless first == 32_u8 || first == 9_u8 || last == 32_u8 || last == 9_u8
    HTTP.trim(value)
  end

  # *value* added to the field *name*: a second line joined to the first
  # as HTTP/1.1 joins them, cookies with "; " (§8.2.3).
  def self.join(headers : Hash(String, String), name : String, value : String) : Nil
    if had = headers[name]?
      headers[name] = had + (name == "cookie" ? "; " : ", ") + value
    else
      headers[name] = value
    end
  end

  def self.field(fields : Array(Std::Hpack::HeaderField), name : String) : String?
    fields.each { |f| return f.value if f.name == name }
    nil
  end

  def self.setting(name : Int32, value : Int32) : String
    String.new(6) do |b|
      b[0] = ((name >> 8) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[1] = (name & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[2] = ((value >> 24) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[3] = ((value >> 16) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[4] = ((value >> 8) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[5] = (value & 0xFF).to_u8
      nil
    end
  end

  def self.u32(value : Int32) : String
    String.new(4) do |b|
      b[0] = ((value >> 24) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[1] = ((value >> 16) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[2] = ((value >> 8) & 0xFF).to_u8
      b[3] = (value & 0xFF).to_u8
      nil
    end
  end

  def self.read_u32(text : String, at : Int32) : Int32
    p = text.to_unsafe
    (p[at].to_i32 << 24) | (p[at + 1].to_i32 << 16) | (p[at + 2].to_i32 << 8) | p[at + 3].to_i32
  end

  def self.error_name(code : Int32) : String
    case code
    when  0 then "NO_ERROR"
    when  1 then "PROTOCOL_ERROR"
    when  2 then "INTERNAL_ERROR"
    when  3 then "FLOW_CONTROL_ERROR"
    when  4 then "SETTINGS_TIMEOUT"
    when  5 then "STREAM_CLOSED"
    when  6 then "FRAME_SIZE_ERROR"
    when  7 then "REFUSED_STREAM"
    when  8 then "CANCEL"
    when  9 then "COMPRESSION_ERROR"
    when 10 then "CONNECT_ERROR"
    when 11 then "ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM"
    when 12 then "INADEQUATE_SECURITY"
    when 13 then "HTTP_1_1_REQUIRED"
    else         "error #{code}"
    end
  end
end

# What a `BodyWriter` writes to for an HTTP/2 answer: DATA frames on one
# stream, as its windows allow.
class H2Out
  def initialize(@h2 : H2Connection, @id : Int32)
  end

  # Each write goes out at once: a block writing as it goes (events, a
  # progress line) is read as it writes.
  def write(text : String) : Int32 | Cancelled | SocketError
    return text.bytesize if @h2.send_data(@id, text, false) && @h2.flush
    fault = @h2.failure
    return fault if fault.is_a?(Cancelled) || fault.is_a?(SocketError)
    SocketError.layered("write an HTTP/2 answer", fault ? fault.message : "the client reset the stream")
  end
end
